@heading Study guide (added for this edition)

The shell connects small programs through arguments, streams, and exit status. This lecture starts with paths and command discovery, then combines text tools into pipelines. It ends with Bash control flow and exercises that turn commands into reusable scripts. Read each example as a data flow: identify the input, the operation, and the output before you run it.

@subheading Learning objectives
@itemize @bullet
@item
Navigate with absolute and relative paths, and explain the current working directory.
@item
Use documentation to inspect command options and locate programs.
@item
Combine grep, sed, awk, sort, and uniq through pipes and redirections.
@item
Read Bash variables, conditions, loops, and background jobs.
@end itemize

@subheading Reading notes
@itemize @bullet
@item
Console examples include a prompt and sample output. Enter only the command after the prompt. The displayed date is an example.
@item
Bash can run builtins such as echo before it searches PATH. Use type or command -V to inspect command resolution; which alone can miss builtins and aliases.
@item
The for-loop fragment is incomplete by design. Add its body and done before use. The stress example also needs the stress program and a suitable Rust project.
@item
The sed -i form shown uses GNU syntax. Check your local sed manual before an in-place edit on another system.
@end itemize

