@node Shell - What is the shell?, Shell - Why should you care about it?, Shell - Topic 1 The Shell, Shell - Topic 1 The Shell
@unnumberedsubsec What is the shell?
@cindex What is the shell?

Computers these days have a variety of interfaces for giving them commands; fanciful graphical user interfaces, voice interfaces, AR/VR, and more recently: LLMs. These are great for 80% of use-cases, but they are often fundamentally restricted in what they allow you to do --- you cannot press a button that isn't there or give a voice command that hasn't been programmed. To take full advantage of the tools your computer provides, we have to go old-school and drop down to a textual interface: The Shell.

Nearly all platforms you can get your hands on have a shell in one form or another, and many of them have several shells for you to choose from. While they may vary in the details, at their core they are all roughly the same: they allow you to run programs, give them input, and inspect their output in a semi-structured way.

To open a shell @emph{prompt} (where you can type commands), you first need a @emph{terminal}, which is the visual interface to a shell. Your device probably shipped with one installed, or you can install one fairly easily:

@itemize
@item
@strong{Linux:} Press @code{Ctrl + Alt + T} (works on most distributions). Or search for "Terminal" in your applications menu.
@item
@strong{Windows:} Press @code{Win + R}, type @code{cmd} or @code{powershell}, and press Enter. Alternatively, search "Terminal" or "Command Prompt" in the Start menu.
@item
@strong{macOS:} Press @code{Cmd + Space} to open Spotlight, type "Terminal", and press Enter. Or find it in Applications → Utilities → Terminal.
@end itemize

On Linux and macOS, this will usually open the Bourne Again SHell, or "bash" for short. This is one of the most widely used shells, and its syntax is similar to what you will see in many other shells. On Windows, you'll be greeted by the "batch" or "powershell" shells, depending on which command you ran. These are Windows-specific, and not what we'll be focusing on in this class, although it has analogues for most of what we'll be teaching. You'll instead want the @uref{https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/wsl/,Windows Subsystem for Linux} or a Linux virtual machine.

Other shells exist, often with many ergonomic improvements over bash (fish and zsh are among the most common). While these are very popular (all the instructors use one), they're nowhere near as ubiquitous as bash, and lean on many of the same concepts, so we won't be focusing on those in this lecture.

@node Shell - Why should you care about it?, Shell - Navigating in the shell, Shell - What is the shell?, Shell - Topic 1 The Shell
@unnumberedsubsec Why should you care about it?
@cindex Why should you care about it?

The shell is not just (usually) much faster than "clicking around", it also comes with expressive power you can't easily find in any one graphical program. As we'll see, the shell gives you the ability to @emph{combine} programs in creative ways to automate nearly any task.

Knowing your way around a shell is also very useful to navigate the world of open-source software (which often come with install instructions that require the shell), building continuous integration for your software projects (as described in the @ref{Quality,,Code Quality lecture}), and debugging errors when other programs fail.

@node Shell - Navigating in the shell, Shell - What is available in the shell?, Shell - Why should you care about it?, Shell - Topic 1 The Shell
@unnumberedsubsec Navigating in the shell
@cindex Navigating in the shell

When you launch your terminal, you will see a @emph{prompt} that often looks a little like this:

@example
missing:~$
@end example

This is the main textual interface to the shell. It tells you that you are on the machine @code{missing} and that your "current working directory", or where you currently are, is @code{~} (short for "home"). The @code{$} tells you that you are not the root user (more on that later). At this prompt you can type a @emph{command}, which will then be interpreted by the shell. The most basic command is to execute a program:

@example
missing:~$ date
Fri 10 Jan 2020 11:49:31 AM EST
missing:~$
@end example

Here, we executed the @code{date} program, which (perhaps unsurprisingly) prints the current date and time. The shell then asks us for another command to execute. We can also execute a command with @emph{arguments}:

@example
missing:~$ echo hello
hello
@end example

In this case, we told the shell to execute the program @code{echo} with the argument @code{hello}. The @code{echo} program simply prints out its arguments. The shell parses the command by splitting it by whitespace, and then runs the program indicated by the first word, supplying each subsequent word as an argument that the program can access. If you want to provide an argument that contains spaces or other special characters (e.g., a directory named "My Photos"), you can either quote the argument with @code{'} or @code{"} (@code{"My Photos"}), or escape just the relevant characters with @code{\} (@code{My\ Photos}).

Perhaps the most important command when you're starting out is @code{man}, short for "manual". The @code{man} program, among other things, lets you look up more information about any command on your system. For example, if you run @code{man date}, it'll explain what @code{date} is, and all of the various arguments you can pass it to alter its behavior. You can also usually get a short version of the help by passing @code{--help} as an argument to most commands.

@quotation
Consider installing and using @uref{https://tldr.sh/,tldr} in addition to @code{man}, as it shows you common usage examples right there in the terminal. LLMs are also usually very good at explaining how commands work and how you can call them to achieve what you want to accomplish.
@end quotation
After @code{man}, the most important command to learn is @code{cd}, or "change directory". This command is actually built into the shell, and isn't a separate program (i.e., @code{which cd} will say "no cd found"). You pass it a path, and that path becomes your current working directory. You'll also see the working directory reflected in the shell prompt:

@example
missing:~$ cd /bin
missing:/bin$ cd /
missing:/$ cd ~
missing:~$
@end example

@quotation
Note that the shell comes with auto-completion, so you can often complete paths faster by pressing @code{<TAB>}!
@end quotation
A lot of commands operate on the current working directory if nothing else is specified. If you're ever unsure where you are, you can run @code{pwd} or print the @code{$PWD} environment variable (with @code{echo $PWD}), both of which produce the current working directory.

The current working directory also comes in handy in that it allows us to use @emph{relative} paths. All the paths we've seen so far have been @emph{absolute} --- they start with @code{/} and give the full set of directories needed to navigate to some location from the root of the file system (@code{/}). In practice, you'll more commonly work with relative paths; so called because they are relative to the current working directory. In a relative path (anything @emph{not} starting with @code{/}), the first path component is looked up in the current working directory, and subsequent components traverse as usual. For example:

@example
missing:~$ cd /
missing:/$ cd bin
missing:/bin$
@end example

There are also two "special" components that exist in every directory: @code{.} and @code{..}. @code{.} is "this directory", and @code{..} is "the parent directory". So:

@example
missing:~$ cd /
missing:/$ cd bin/../bin/../bin/././../bin/..
missing:/$
@end example

You can usually use absolute and relative paths interchangeably for any command argument, just keep in mind what your current working directory is when using a relative one!

@quotation
Consider installing and using @uref{https://github.com/ajeetdsouza/zoxide,zoxide} to speed up your @code{cd}ing --- @code{z} will remember the paths you frequently visit and let you access with less typing.
@end quotation

@node Shell - What is available in the shell?, Shell - The shell language bash, Shell - Navigating in the shell, Shell - Topic 1 The Shell
@unnumberedsubsec What is available in the shell?
@cindex What is available in the shell?

But how does the shell know how to find programs like @code{date} or @code{echo}? If the shell is asked to execute a command, it consults an @emph{environment variable} called @code{$PATH} that lists which directories the shell should search for programs when it is given a command:

@example
missing:~$ echo $PATH
/usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin
missing:~$ which echo
/bin/echo
missing:~$ /bin/echo $PATH
/usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin
@end example

When we run the @code{echo} command, the shell sees that it should execute the program @code{echo}, and then searches through the @code{:}-separated list of directories in @code{$PATH} for a file by that name. When it finds it, it runs it (assuming the file is @emph{executable}; more on that later). We can find out which file is executed for a given program name using the @code{which} program. We can also bypass @code{$PATH} entirely by giving the @emph{path} to the file we want to execute.

This also gives a clue for how we can determine @emph{all} the programs we're able to execute in the shell: by listing the contents of all the directories on @code{$PATH}. We can do this by passing a given directory path to the @code{ls} program, which lists files:

@example
missing:~$ ls /bin
@end example

@quotation
Consider installing and using @uref{https://eza.rocks/,eza} for a more human-friendly @code{ls}.
@end quotation
This will, on most computers, print a @emph{lot} of programs, but we'll only focus on some of the most important ones here. First, some simple ones:

@itemize
@item
@code{cat file}, which prints the contents of @code{file}.
@item
@code{sort file}, which prints out the lines of @code{file} in sorted order.
@item
@code{uniq file}, which eliminates consecutive duplicate lines from @code{file}.
@item
@code{head file} and @code{tail file}, which respectively print the first and last few lines of @code{file}.
@end itemize

@quotation
Consider installing and using @uref{https://github.com/sharkdp/bat,bat} over @code{cat} for syntax highlighting and scrolling.
@end quotation
There's also @code{grep pattern file}, which finds lines matching @code{pattern} in @code{file}. This one deserves slightly more attention as it's both @emph{very} useful and sports a wider array of features than one may expect. @code{pattern} is actually a @emph{regular expression} which can express very complex patterns --- we'll @ref{Quality - Regular expressions,,cover those} in the code quality lecture. You can also specify a directory instead of a file (or leave it off for @code{.}) and pass @code{-r} to recursively search all the files in a directory.

@quotation
Consider installing and using @uref{https://github.com/BurntSushi/ripgrep,ripgrep} over @code{grep} for a faster and more human-friendly (but less portable) alternative. @code{ripgrep} will also recursively search the current working directory by default!
@end quotation
There are also some very useful tools with a slightly more complicated interface. First among those is @code{sed}, which is a programmatic file editor. It has its own programming language for making automated edits to files, but the most common use of it is:

@example
missing:~$ sed -i 's/pattern/replacement/g' file
@end example

This replaces all instances of @code{pattern} with @code{replacement} in @code{file}. The @code{-i} indicates that we want the substitutions to happen inline (as opposed to leaving @code{file} unmodified and printing the substituted contents). The @code{s/} is the way to express in the sed programming language that we want to do a substitution. The @code{/} separates the pattern from the replacement. And the trailing @code{/g} indicates that we want to replace @emph{all} occurrences on each line rather than just the first. As with @code{grep}, @code{pattern} here is a regular expression, which gives you significant expressive power. Regular expression substitutions also allow @code{replacement} to refer back to parts of the matched pattern; we'll see an example of that in a second.

Next, we have @code{find}, which lets you find files (recursively) that match certain conditions. For example:

@example
missing:~$ find ~/Downloads -type f -name "*.zip" -mtime +30
@end example

Finds ZIP files in the download directory that are older than 30 days.

@example
missing:~$ find ~ -type f -size +100M -exec ls -lh @{@} \;
@end example

Finds files larger than 100M in your home directory and lists them. Note that @code{-exec} takes a @emph{command} terminated with a stand-alone @code{;} (which we need to escape much like a space) where @code{@{@}} is replaced with each matching file path by @code{find}.

@example
missing:~$ find . -name "*.py" -exec grep -l "TODO" @{@} \;
@end example

Finds any @code{.py} files with TODO items in them.

The syntax of @code{find} can be a little daunting, but hopefully this gives you a sense of how useful it can be!

@quotation
Consider installing and using @uref{https://github.com/sharkdp/fd,fd} instead of @code{find} for a more human-friendly (but less portable!) experience.
@end quotation
Next on the docket is @code{awk}, which, like @code{sed}, has its own programming language. Where @code{sed} is built for editing files, @code{awk} is built for parsing them. By far the most common use of @code{awk} is for data files with a regular syntax (like CSV files) where you want to extract only certain parts of every record (i.e., line):

@example
missing:~$ awk '@{print $2@}' file
@end example

Prints the second whitespace-separated column of every line of @code{file}. If you add @code{-F,}, it'll print the second comma-separated column of every line. @code{awk} can do much more --- filtering rows, computing aggregates, and more --- see the exercises for a taste.

Putting these tools together, we can do fancy things like:

@example
missing:~$ ssh myserver 'journalctl -u sshd -b-1 | grep "Disconnected from"' \
  | sed -E 's/.*Disconnected from .* user (.*) [^ ]+ port.*/\1/' \
  | sort | uniq -c \
  | sort -nk1,1 | tail -n10 \
  | awk '@{print $2@}' | paste -sd,
postgres,mysql,oracle,dell,ubuntu,inspur,test,admin,user,root
@end example

This grabs SSH logs from a remote server (we'll talk more about @code{ssh} in the next lecture), searches for disconnect messages, extracts the username from each such message, and prints the top 10 usernames comma-separated. All in one command! We'll leave dissecting each step as an exercise.

@node Shell - The shell language bash, , Shell - What is available in the shell?, Shell - Topic 1 The Shell
@unnumberedsubsec The shell language (bash)
@cindex The shell language (bash)

The previous example introduced a new concept: pipes (@code{|}). These let you string together the output of one program with the input of another. This works because most command-line programs will operate on their "standard input" (where your keystrokes normally go) if no @code{file} argument is given. @code{|} takes the "standard output" (what normally gets printed to your terminal) of the program before the @code{|} and makes it be the standard input of the program after the @code{|}. This allows you to @emph{compose} shell programs, and it's part of what makes the shell such a productive environment to work in!

In fact, most shells implement a full programming language (like bash), just like Python or Ruby. It has variables, conditionals, loops, and functions. When you run commands in your shell, you are really writing a small bit of code that your shell interprets. We won't teach you all of bash today, but there are some bits you'll find particularly useful:

First, redirects: @code{>file} lets you take the standard output of a program and write it to @code{file} instead of to your terminal. This makes it easier to analyze after the fact. @code{>>file} will append to @code{file} rather than overwrite it. There's also @code{<file} which tells the shell to read from @code{file} instead of from your keyboard as the standard input to a program.

@quotation
This is a good time to mention the @code{tee} program. @code{tee} will print standard input to standard output (just like @code{cat}!), but will @emph{also} write it to a file. So @code{verbose cmd | tee verbose.log | grep CRITICAL} will preserve the full verbose log to a file while keeping your terminal clean!
@end quotation
Next, conditionals: @code{if command1; then command2; command3; fi} will execute @code{command1}, and if it doesn't result in an error, will run @code{command2} and @code{command3}. You can also have an @code{else} branch if you wish. The most common command to use as @code{command1} is the @code{test} command, often abbreviated simply as @code{[}, which lets you evaluate conditions like "does a file exist" (@code{test -f file} / @code{[ -f file ]}) or "does a string equal another" (@code{[ "$var" = "string" ]}). In bash, there's also @code{[[ ]]}, which is a "safer" built-in version of @code{test} that has fewer odd behaviours around quoting.

Bash also has two forms of loops, @code{while} and @code{for}. @code{while command1; do command2; command3; done} functions just like the equivalent @code{if} command, except that it will re-execute the whole thing over and over for as long as @code{command1} does not error. @code{for varname in a b c d; do command; done} executes @code{command} four times, each time with @code{$varname} set to one of @code{a}, @code{b}, @code{c}, and @code{d}. Instead of listing the items explicitly, you'll often use "command substitution", such as:

@example
for i in $(seq 1 10); do
@end example

This executes the command @code{seq 1 10} (which prints the numbers from 1 to 10 inclusive) and then replaces the whole @code{$()} with that command's output, giving you a 10-iteration for loop. In older code you'll sometimes see literal backticks (like @code{for i in `seq 1 10`; do}) instead of @code{$()}, but you should strongly prefer the @code{$()} form as it can be nested.

While you @emph{can} write long shell scripts directly in your prompt, you'll usually want to write them into a @code{.sh} file instead. For example, here's a script that will run a program in a loop until it fails, printing the output only of the failed run, while stressing your CPU in the background (useful to reproduce flaky tests for example):

@example
#!/bin/bash
set -euo pipefail

# Start CPU stress in background
stress --cpu 8 &
STRESS_PID=$!

# Setup log file
LOGFILE="test_runs_$(date +%s).log"
echo "Logging to $LOGFILE"

# Run tests until one fails
RUN=1
while cargo test my_test > "$LOGFILE" 2>&1; do
    echo "Run $RUN passed"
    ((RUN++))
done

# Cleanup and report
kill $STRESS_PID
echo "Test failed on run $RUN"
echo "Last 20 lines of output:"
tail -n 20 "$LOGFILE"
echo "Full log: $LOGFILE"
@end example

This has a number of new things in it that I recommend you spend some time diving into, as they're very useful in crafting useful shell invocations like background jobs (@code{&}) to run programs concurrently, trickier @uref{https://www.gnu.org/software/bash/manual/html_node/Redirections.html,shell redirections}, and @uref{https://www.gnu.org/software/bash/manual/html_node/Arithmetic-Expansion.html,arithmetic expansion}.

It's worth spending a second on the first two lines of the program though. The first is the "shebang" -- you'll see this at the top of other files than shell scripts too. When a file that starts with the magic incantation @code{#!/path} is executed, the shell will start the program at @code{/path}, and pass it the contents of the file as input. In the case of a shell script, this means passing the contents of the shell script to @code{/bin/bash}, but you can also write Python scripts with a shebang line of @code{/usr/bin/python}!

The second line is a way to make bash "stricter", and mitigate a number of footguns when writing shell scripts. @code{set} can take a whole lot of arguments, but briefly: @code{-e} makes it so that if any command fails, the script exits early; @code{-u} makes it so that use of undefined variables crashes the script rather than just using an empty string; and @code{-o pipefail} makes it so that if programs in a @code{|} sequence fail, the shell script as a whole also exits early.

@quotation
Shell programming is a deep topic, just as any programming language is, but be warned: bash has an unusual number of gotchas, to the point that there are @uref{https://tldp.org/LDP/abs/html/gotchas.html,multiple} websites dedicated to @uref{https://mywiki.wooledge.org/BashPitfalls,listing them}. I highly recommend making heavy use of @uref{https://www.shellcheck.net/,shellcheck} when writing them. LLMs are also great at writing and debugging shell scripts, as well as translating them to a "real" programming language (like Python) when they've grown too unwieldy for bash (100+ lines).
@end quotation

