@unnumberedsec Next steps
@cindex Next steps

At this point you know your way around a shell enough to accomplish basic tasks. You should be able to navigate around to find files of interest and use the basic functionality of most programs. In the next lecture, we will talk about how to perform and automate more complex tasks using the shell and the many handy command-line programs out there.

@node Shell - Exercises, , Shell - Next steps, Shell
@unnumberedsec Exercises
@cindex Exercises

All classes in this course are accompanied by a series of exercises. Some give you a specific task to do, while others are open-ended, like "try using X and Y programs". We highly encourage you to try them out.

We have not written solutions for the exercises. If you are stuck on anything in particular, feel free to post in @code{#missing-semester-forum} on @uref{https://ossu.dev/#community,Discord} or send us an email describing what you've tried so far, and we will try to help you out. These exercises will also likely work well as initial prompts in a conversation with an LLM where you can interactively dive into the topic. The real value in these exercises is the journey of discovering the answers, not the answer itself. We encourage you to follow tangents and ask "why" as you work through them, rather than just looking for the shortest path to the solution.

@enumerate
@item
For this course, you need to be using a Unix shell like Bash or ZSH. If you are on Linux or macOS, you don't have to do anything special. If you are on Windows, you need to make sure you are not running cmd.exe or PowerShell; you can use @uref{https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/wsl/,Windows Subsystem for Linux} or a Linux virtual machine to use Unix-style command-line tools. To make sure you're running an appropriate shell, you can try the command @code{echo $SHELL}. If it says something like @code{/bin/bash} or @code{/usr/bin/zsh}, that means you're running the right program.

@item
What does the @code{-l} flag to @code{ls} do? Run @code{ls -l /} and examine the output. What do the first 10 characters of each line mean? (Hint: @code{man ls})

@item
In the command @code{find ~/Downloads -type f -name "*.zip" -mtime +30}, the @code{*.zip} is a "glob". What is a glob? Create a test directory with some files and experiment with patterns like @code{ls *.txt}, @code{ls file?.txt}, and @code{ls @{a,b,c@}.txt}. See @uref{https://www.gnu.org/software/bash/manual/html_node/Pattern-Matching.html,Pattern Matching} in the Bash manual.

@item
What's the difference between @code{'single quotes'}, @code{"double quotes"}, and @code{$'ANSI quotes'}? Write a command that echoes a string containing a literal @code{$}, a @code{!}, and a newline character. See @uref{https://www.gnu.org/software/bash/manual/html_node/Quoting.html,Quoting}.

@item
The shell has three standard streams: stdin (0), stdout (1), and stderr (2). Run @code{ls /nonexistent /tmp} and redirect stdout to one file and stderr to another. How would you redirect both to the same file? See @uref{https://www.gnu.org/software/bash/manual/html_node/Redirections.html,Redirections}.

@item
@code{$?} holds the exit status of the last command (0 = success). @code{&&} runs the next command only if the previous succeeded; @code{||} runs it only if the previous failed. Write a one-liner that creates @code{/tmp/mydir} only if it doesn't already exist. See @uref{https://www.gnu.org/software/bash/manual/html_node/Exit-Status.html,Exit Status}.

@item
Why does @code{cd} have to be built into the shell itself rather than a standalone program? (Hint: think about what a child process can and cannot affect in its parent.)

@item
Write a script that takes a filename as an argument (@code{$1}) and checks whether the file exists using @code{test -f} or @code{[ -f ... ]}. It should print different messages depending on whether the file exists. See @uref{https://www.gnu.org/software/bash/manual/html_node/Bash-Conditional-Expressions.html,Bash Conditional Expressions}.

@item
Save the script from the previous exercise to a file (e.g., @code{check.sh}). Try running it with @code{./check.sh somefile}. What happens? Now run @code{chmod +x check.sh} and try again. Why is this step necessary? (Hint: look at @code{ls -l check.sh} before and after the @code{chmod}.)

@item
What happens if you add @code{-x} to the @code{set} flags in a script? Try it with a simple script and observe the output. See @uref{https://www.gnu.org/software/bash/manual/html_node/The-Set-Builtin.html,The Set Builtin}.

@item
Write a command that copies a file to a backup with today's date in the filename (e.g., @code{notes.txt} → @code{notes_2026-01-12.txt}). (Hint: @code{$(date +%Y-%m-%d)}). See @uref{https://www.gnu.org/software/bash/manual/html_node/Command-Substitution.html,Command Substitution}.

@item
Modify the flaky test script from the lecture to accept the test command as an argument instead of hardcoding @code{cargo test my_test}. (Hint: @code{$1} or @code{$@@}). See @uref{https://www.gnu.org/software/bash/manual/html_node/Special-Parameters.html,Special Parameters}.

@item
Use pipes to find the 5 most common file extensions in your home directory. (Hint: combine @code{find}, @code{grep} or @code{sed} or @code{awk}, @code{sort}, @code{uniq -c}, and @code{head}.)

@item
@code{xargs} converts lines from stdin into command arguments. Use @code{find} and @code{xargs} together (not @code{find -exec}) to find all @code{.sh} files in a directory and count the lines in each with @code{wc -l}. Bonus: make it handle filenames with spaces. (Hint: @code{-print0} and @code{-0}). See @code{man xargs}.

@item
Use @code{curl} to fetch the HTML of the course website (@code{https://missing.csail.mit.edu/}) and pipe it to @code{grep} to count how many lectures are listed. (Hint: look for a pattern that appears once per lecture; use @code{curl -s} to silence the progress output.)

@item
@uref{https://jqlang.github.io/jq/,jq} is a powerful tool for processing JSON data. Fetch the sample data at @code{https://microsoftedge.github.io/Demos/json-dummy-data/64KB.json} with @code{curl} and use @code{jq} to extract just the names of people whose version is greater than 6. (Hint: pipe to @code{jq .} first to see the structure; then try @code{jq '.[] | select(...) | .name'})

@item
@code{awk} can filter lines based on column values and manipulate output. For example, @code{awk '$3 ~ /pattern/ @{$4=""; print@}'} prints only lines where the third column matches @code{pattern}, while omitting the fourth column. Write an @code{awk} command that prints only lines where the second column is greater than 100, and swaps the first and third columns. Test with: @code{printf 'a 50 x\nb 150 y\nc 200 z\n'}

@item
Dissect the SSH log pipeline from the lecture: what does each step do? Then build something similar to find your most-used shell commands from @code{~/.bash_history} (or @code{~/.zsh_history}).

@end enumerate

