@heading Study guide (added for this edition)

Command-line tools share conventions that make them work together. This lecture explains arguments, file streams, environment variables, exit status, and signals. It then applies those concepts to SSH, tmux, shell configuration, and terminal tools. Track which process owns each operation. In an SSH pipeline, a change in quoting can move work between the local computer and the remote computer.

@subheading Learning objectives
@itemize @bullet
@item
Distinguish shell expansion from the arguments that a program receives.
@item
Redirect standard input, standard output, and standard error separately.
@item
Use exit status, signals, and job control to manage processes.
@item
Configure remote connections and keep work in a tmux session.
@item
Manage shell aliases and dotfiles with version control.
@end itemize

@subheading Reading notes
@itemize @bullet
@item
Quote argument expansions such as "$1" and "$@@" when each value must remain one argument. The opening script is a teaching example: if $DEBUG executes its value as a command.
@item
A shell script normally returns the status of its last command. An explicit exit selects another status. A nonzero grep status can mean no match or an error.
@item
The ls-based deletion loop does not handle all filenames. Study expansion with harmless files before using a deletion command.
@item
Terminal key sequences describe separate key presses. For tmux, press and release Ctrl-b, then press the next key.
@end itemize

@heading Original lecture notes

As we covered in the previous lecture, most shells are not a mere launcher to start up other programs, but in practice they provide an entire programming language full of common patterns and abstractions. However, unlike the majority of programming languages, in shell scripting everything is designed around running programs and getting them to communicate with each other simply and efficiently.

In particular, shell scripting is tightly bound by @emph{conventions}. For a command line interface (CLI) program to play nicely within the broader shell environment there are some common patterns that it needs to follow. We will now cover many of the concepts required to understand how command line programs work as well as ubiquitous conventions on how to use and configure them.

