@unnumberedsubsec Arguments and Globs
@cindex Arguments and Globs

@enumerate
@item
You might see commands like @code{cmd --flag -- --notaflag}. The @code{--} is a special argument that tells the program to stop parsing flags. Everything after @code{--} is treated as a positional argument. Why might this be useful? Try running @code{touch -- -myfile} and then removing it without @code{--}.

@item
Read @uref{https://www.man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/ls.1.html,man ls} and write an @code{ls} command that lists files in the following manner:

@itemize
@item
Includes all files, including hidden files
@item
Sizes are listed in human readable format (e.g. 454M instead of 454279954)
@item
Files are ordered by recency
@item
Output is colorized
@end itemize

A sample output would look like this:

@example
-rw-r--r--   1 user group 1.1M Jan 14 09:53 baz
drwxr-xr-x   5 user group  160 Jan 14 09:53 .
-rw-r--r--   1 user group  514 Jan 14 06:42 bar
-rw-r--r--   1 user group 106M Jan 13 12:12 foo
drwx------+ 47 user group 1.5K Jan 12 18:08 ..
@end example

@item
Process substitution @code{<(command)} lets you use a command's output as if it were a file. Use @code{diff} with process substitution to compare the output of @code{printenv} and @code{export}. Why are they different? (Hint: try @code{diff <(printenv | sort) <(export | sort)}).

@end enumerate

@node CLI - Environment Variables, CLI - Return Codes, CLI - Arguments and Globs, CLI - Exercises
@unnumberedsubsec Environment Variables
@cindex Environment Variables

@enumerate
@item
Write bash functions @code{marco} and @code{polo} that do the following: whenever you execute @code{marco} the current working directory should be saved in some manner, then when you execute @code{polo}, no matter what directory you are in, @code{polo} should @code{cd} you back to the directory where you executed @code{marco}. For ease of debugging you can write the code in a file @code{marco.sh} and (re)load the definitions to your shell by executing @code{source marco.sh}.
@end enumerate

@node CLI - Return Codes, CLI - Signals and Job Control, CLI - Environment Variables, CLI - Exercises
@unnumberedsubsec Return Codes
@cindex Return Codes

@enumerate
@item
Say you have a command that fails rarely. In order to debug it you need to capture its output but it can be time consuming to get a failure run. Write a bash script that runs the following script until it fails and captures its standard output and error streams to files and prints everything at the end. Bonus points if you can also report how many runs it took for the script to fail.

@example
#!/usr/bin/env bash

n=$(( RANDOM % 100 ))

if [[ $n -eq 42 ]]; then
   echo "Something went wrong"
   >&2 echo "The error was using magic numbers"
   exit 1
fi

echo "Everything went according to plan"
@end example

@end enumerate

@node CLI - Signals and Job Control, CLI - Files and Permissions, CLI - Return Codes, CLI - Exercises
@unnumberedsubsec Signals and Job Control
@cindex Signals and Job Control

@enumerate
@item
Start a @code{sleep 10000} job in a terminal, background it with @code{Ctrl-Z} and continue its execution with @code{bg}. Now use @uref{https://www.man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/pgrep.1.html,pgrep} to find its pid and @uref{https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/pgrep.1.html,pkill} to kill it without ever typing the pid itself. (Hint: use the @code{-lf} flags).

@item
Say you don't want to start a process until another completes. How would you go about it? In this exercise, our limiting process will always be @code{sleep 60 &}. One way to achieve this is to use the @uref{https://www.man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/wait.1p.html,wait} command. Try launching the sleep command and having an @code{ls} wait until the background process finishes.

However, this strategy will fail if we start in a different bash session, since @code{wait} only works for child processes. One feature we did not discuss in the notes is that the @code{kill} command's exit status will be zero on success and nonzero otherwise. @code{kill -0} does not send a signal but will give a nonzero exit status if the process does not exist. Write a bash function called @code{pidwait} that takes a pid and waits until the given process completes. You should use @code{sleep} to avoid wasting CPU unnecessarily.

@end enumerate

@node CLI - Files and Permissions, CLI - Terminal Multiplexers - more, CLI - Signals and Job Control, CLI - Exercises
@unnumberedsubsec Files and Permissions
@cindex Files and Permissions

@enumerate
@item
(Advanced) Write a command or script to recursively find the most recently modified file in a directory. More generally, can you list all files by recency?
@end enumerate

@node CLI - Terminal Multiplexers - more, CLI - Aliases and Dotfiles, CLI - Files and Permissions, CLI - Exercises
@unnumberedsubsec Terminal Multiplexers
@cindex Terminal Multiplexers

@enumerate
@item
Follow this @code{tmux} @uref{https://www.hamvocke.com/blog/a-quick-and-easy-guide-to-tmux/,tutorial} and then learn how to do some basic customizations following @uref{https://www.hamvocke.com/blog/a-guide-to-customizing-your-tmux-conf/,these steps}.
@end enumerate

@node CLI - Aliases and Dotfiles, CLI - Remote Machines SSH, CLI - Terminal Multiplexers - more, CLI - Exercises
@unnumberedsubsec Aliases and Dotfiles
@cindex Aliases and Dotfiles

@enumerate
@item
Create an alias @code{dc} that resolves to @code{cd} for when you type it wrong.

@item
Run @code{history | awk '@{$1="";print substr($0,2)@}' | sort | uniq -c | sort -n | tail -n 10} to get your top 10 most used commands and consider writing shorter aliases for them. Note: this works for Bash; if you're using ZSH, use @code{history 1} instead of just @code{history}.

@item
Create a folder for your dotfiles and set up version control.

@item
Add a configuration for at least one program, e.g. your shell, with some customization (to start off, it can be something as simple as customizing your shell prompt by setting @code{$PS1}).

@item
Set up a method to install your dotfiles quickly (and without manual effort) on a new machine. This can be as simple as a shell script that calls @code{ln -s} for each file, or you could use a @uref{https://dotfiles.github.io/utilities/,specialized utility}.

@item
Test your installation script on a fresh virtual machine.

@item
Migrate all of your current tool configurations to your dotfiles repository.

@item
Publish your dotfiles on GitHub.

@end enumerate

@node CLI - Remote Machines SSH, , CLI - Aliases and Dotfiles, CLI - Exercises
@unnumberedsubsec Remote Machines (SSH)
@cindex Remote Machines (SSH)

Install a Linux virtual machine (or use an already existing one) for these exercises. If you are not familiar with virtual machines check out @uref{https://hibbard.eu/install-ubuntu-virtual-box/,this} tutorial for installing one.

@enumerate
@item
Go to @code{~/.ssh/} and check if you have a pair of SSH keys there. If not, generate them with @code{ssh-keygen -a 100 -t ed25519}. It is recommended that you use a password and use @code{ssh-agent}, more info @uref{https://www.ssh.com/ssh/agent,here}.

@item
Edit @code{.ssh/config} to have an entry as follows:

@example
Host vm
    User username_goes_here
    HostName ip_goes_here
    IdentityFile ~/.ssh/id_ed25519
    LocalForward 9999 localhost:8888
@end example

@item
Use @code{ssh-copy-id vm} to copy your ssh key to the server.

@item
Start a webserver in your VM by executing @code{python -m http.server 8888}. Access the VM webserver by navigating to @code{http://localhost:9999} in your machine.

@item
Edit your SSH server config by doing @code{sudo vim /etc/ssh/sshd_config} and disable password authentication by editing the value of @code{PasswordAuthentication}. Disable root login by editing the value of @code{PermitRootLogin}. Restart the @code{ssh} service with @code{sudo service sshd restart}. Try sshing in again.

@item
(Challenge) Install @uref{https://mosh.org/,mosh} in the VM and establish a connection. Then disconnect the network adapter of the server/VM. Can mosh properly recover from it?

@item
(Challenge) Look into what the @code{-N} and @code{-f} flags do in @code{ssh} and figure out a command to achieve background port forwarding.

@end enumerate

