@heading Study guide (added for this edition)

A development environment supports reading, navigation, edits, and checks. This lecture combines three ideas: Vim treats editing as a small language; language servers connect code meaning to editor features; AI tools propose code through completion, inline edits, and agents. Start with a small working setup. Add features when you can explain how they improve your normal work.

@subheading Learning objectives
@itemize @bullet
@item
Change Vim modes and combine motions, operators, counts, and text objects.
@item
Use code navigation, documentation, formatting, and type information in an editor.
@item
Point the language server at the project environment and its dependencies.
@item
Distinguish AI completion from inline edits and agent work.
@item
Evaluate extensions for remote work, containers, and collaborative editing.
@end itemize

@subheading Reading notes
@itemize @bullet
@item
The Fizz Buzz program is deliberately broken. Preserve it while you follow the editing exercise. Key sequences assume the stated file layout and Normal mode.
@item
AI completion examples show separate stages. Some blocks are function bodies or unfinished definitions, so they are not independent runnable programs.
@item
The link-extraction expression demonstrates prompting. It does not parse every Markdown link form, including reference-style links used in this lecture.
@item
The requests example omits a timeout and HTTP error check. Use a Markdown parser and explicit network error handling for a maintained downloader.
@end itemize

@heading Original lecture notes

A @emph{development environment} is a set of tools for developing software. At the heart of a development environment is text editing functionality, along with accompanying features such as syntax highlighting, type checking, code formatting, and autocomplete. @emph{Integrated development environments} (IDEs) such as @uref{https://code.visualstudio.com/,VS Code} bring together all of this functionality into a single application. Terminal-based development workflows combine tools such as @uref{https://github.com/tmux/tmux,tmux} (a terminal multiplexer), @uref{https://www.vim.org/,Vim} (a text editor), @uref{https://www.zsh.org/,Zsh} (a shell), and language-specific command-line tools, such as @uref{https://docs.astral.sh/ruff/,Ruff} (a Python linter and code formatter) and @uref{https://mypy-lang.org/,Mypy} (a Python type checker).

IDEs and terminal-based workflows each have their strengths and weaknesses. For example, graphical IDEs can be easier to learn, and today's IDEs generally have better out-of-the-box AI integrations like AI autocomplete; on the other hand, terminal-based workflows are lightweight, and they may be your only option in environments where you don't have a GUI or can't install software. We recommend you develop basic familiarity with both and develop mastery of at least one. If you don't already have a preferred IDE, we recommend starting with @uref{https://code.visualstudio.com/,VS Code}.

In this lecture, we'll cover:

