@node Development - Text editing and Vim, Development - Code intelligence and language servers, Development, Development
@unnumberedsec Text editing and Vim
@cindex Text editing and Vim

When programming, you spend most of your time navigating through code, reading snippets of code, and making edits to code, rather than writing long streams or reading files top-to-bottom. @uref{https://www.vim.org/,Vim} is a text editor that is optimized for this distribution of tasks.

@strong{The philosophy of Vim.} Vim has a beautiful idea as its foundation: its interface is itself a programming language, designed for navigating and editing text. Keystrokes (with mnemonic names) are commands, and these commands are composable. Vim avoids the use of the mouse, because it's too slow; Vim even avoids use of the arrow keys because it requires too much movement. The result: an editor that feels like a brain-computer interface and matches the speed at which you think.

@strong{Vim support in other software.} You don't have to use @uref{https://www.vim.org/,Vim} itself to benefit from the ideas at its core. Many programs that involve any kind of text editing support "Vim mode", either as built-in functionality or as a plugin. For example, VS Code has the @uref{https://marketplace.visualstudio.com/items?itemName=vscodevim.vim,VSCodeVim} plugin, Zsh has @uref{https://zsh.sourceforge.io/Guide/zshguide04.html,built-in support} for Vim emulation, and even Claude Code has @uref{https://code.claude.com/docs/en/interactive-mode#vim-editor-mode,built-in support} for Vim editor mode. Chances are that any tool you use that involves text editing supports Vim mode in one way or another.

@menu
* Development - Modal editing:: Modal editing
* Development - Basics inserting text:: Basics: inserting text
* Development - Vim's interface is a programming language:: Vim's interface is a programming language
* Development - Putting it all together:: Putting it all together
* Development - Learning Vim:: Learning Vim
@end menu

@node Development - Modal editing, Development - Basics inserting text, Development - Text editing and Vim, Development - Text editing and Vim
@unnumberedsubsec Modal editing
@cindex Modal editing

Vim is a @emph{modal editor}: it has different operating modes for different classes of tasks.

@itemize
@item
@strong{Normal}: for moving around a file and making edits
@item
@strong{Insert}: for inserting text
@item
@strong{Replace}: for replacing text
@item
@strong{Visual} (plain, line, or block): for selecting blocks of text
@item
@strong{Command-line}: for running a command
@end itemize

Keystrokes have different meanings in different operating modes. For example, the letter @code{x} in Insert mode will just insert a literal character "x", but in Normal mode, it will delete the character under the cursor, and in Visual mode, it will delete the selection.

In its default configuration, Vim shows the current mode in the bottom left. The initial/default mode is Normal mode. You'll generally spend most of your time between Normal mode and Insert mode.

You change modes by pressing @code{<ESC>} (the escape key) to switch from any mode back to Normal mode. From Normal mode, enter Insert mode with @code{i}, Replace mode with @code{R}, Visual mode with @code{v}, Visual Line mode with @code{V}, Visual Block mode with @code{<C-v>} (Ctrl-V, sometimes also written @code{^V}), and Command-line mode with @code{:}.

You use the @code{<ESC>} key a lot when using Vim: consider remapping Caps Lock to Escape (@uref{https://vim.fandom.com/wiki/Map_caps_lock_to_escape_in_macOS,macOS instructions}) or create an @uref{https://vim.fandom.com/wiki/Avoid_the_escape_key#Mappings,alternative mapping} for @code{<ESC>} with a simple key sequence.

@node Development - Basics inserting text, Development - Vim's interface is a programming language, Development - Modal editing, Development - Text editing and Vim
@unnumberedsubsec Basics: inserting text
@cindex Basics: inserting text

From Normal mode, press @code{i} to enter Insert mode. Now, Vim behaves like any other text editor, until you press @code{<ESC>} to return to Normal mode. This, along with the basics explained above, are all you need to start editing files using Vim (though not particularly efficiently, if you're spending all your time editing from Insert mode).

@node Development - Vim's interface is a programming language, Development - Putting it all together, Development - Basics inserting text, Development - Text editing and Vim
@unnumberedsubsec Vim's interface is a programming language
@cindex Vim's interface is a programming language

Vim's interface is a programming language. Keystrokes (with mnemonic names) are commands, and these commands @emph{compose}. This enables efficient movement and edits, especially once the commands become muscle memory, just like typing becomes super efficient once you've learned your keyboard layout.

@menu
* Development - Movement:: Movement
* Development - Selection:: Selection
* Development - Edits:: Edits
* Development - Counts:: Counts
* Development - Modifiers:: Modifiers
@end menu

@node Development - Movement, Development - Selection, Development - Vim's interface is a programming language, Development - Vim's interface is a programming language
@unnumberedsubsubsec Movement
@cindex Movement

You should spend most of your time in Normal mode, using movement commands to navigate the file. Movements in Vim are also called "nouns", because they refer to chunks of text.

@itemize
@item
Basic movement: @code{hjkl} (left, down, up, right)
@item
Words: @code{w} (next word), @code{b} (beginning of word), @code{e} (end of word)
@item
Lines: @code{0} (beginning of line), @code{^} (first non-blank character), @code{$} (end of line)
@item
Screen: @code{H} (top of screen), @code{M} (middle of screen), @code{L} (bottom of screen)
@item
Scroll: @code{Ctrl-u} (up), @code{Ctrl-d} (down)
@item
File: @code{gg} (beginning of file), @code{G} (end of file)
@item
Line numbers: @code{:@{number@}<CR>} or @code{@{number@}G} (line @{number@})
@itemize
@item
@code{<CR>} refers to the carriage return / enter key
@end itemize

@item
Misc: @code{%} (matching item, like parenthesis or brace)
@item
Find: @code{f@{character@}}, @code{t@{character@}}, @code{F@{character@}}, @code{T@{character@}}
@itemize
@item
find/to forward/backward @{character@} on the current line
@item
@code{,} / @code{;} for navigating matches
@end itemize

@item
Search: @code{/@{regex@}}, @code{n} / @code{N} for navigating matches
@end itemize

@node Development - Selection, Development - Edits, Development - Movement, Development - Vim's interface is a programming language
@unnumberedsubsubsec Selection
@cindex Selection

Visual modes:

@itemize
@item
Visual: @code{v}
@item
Visual Line: @code{V}
@item
Visual Block: @code{Ctrl-v}
@end itemize

Can use movement keys to make selection.

@node Development - Edits, Development - Counts, Development - Selection, Development - Vim's interface is a programming language
@unnumberedsubsubsec Edits
@cindex Edits

Everything that you used to do with the mouse, you now do with the keyboard using editing commands that compose with movement commands. Here's where Vim's interface starts to look like a programming language. Vim's editing commands are also called "verbs", because verbs act on nouns.

@itemize
@item
@code{i} enter Insert mode
@itemize
@item
but for manipulating/deleting text, want to use something more than backspace
@end itemize

@item
@code{o} / @code{O} insert line below / above
@item
@code{d@{motion@}} delete @{motion@}
@itemize
@item
e.g. @code{dw} is delete word, @code{d$} is delete to end of line, @code{d0} is delete to beginning of line
@end itemize

@item
@code{c@{motion@}} change @{motion@}
@itemize
@item
e.g. @code{cw} is change word
@item
like @code{d@{motion@}} followed by @code{i}
@end itemize

@item
@code{x} delete character (equivalent to @code{dl})
@item
@code{s} substitute character (equivalent to @code{cl})
@item
Visual mode + manipulation
@itemize
@item
select text, @code{d} to delete it or @code{c} to change it
@end itemize

@item
@code{u} to undo, @code{<C-r>} to redo
@item
@code{y} to copy / "yank" (some other commands like @code{d} also copy)
@item
@code{p} to paste
@item
Lots more to learn: for example, @code{~} flips the case of a character, and @code{J} joins together lines
@end itemize

@node Development - Counts, Development - Modifiers, Development - Edits, Development - Vim's interface is a programming language
@unnumberedsubsubsec Counts
@cindex Counts

You can combine nouns and verbs with a count, which will perform a given action a number of times.

@itemize
@item
@code{3w} move 3 words forward
@item
@code{5j} move 5 lines down
@item
@code{7dw} delete 7 words
@end itemize

@node Development - Modifiers, , Development - Counts, Development - Vim's interface is a programming language
@unnumberedsubsubsec Modifiers
@cindex Modifiers

You can use modifiers to change the meaning of a noun. Some modifiers are @code{i}, which means "inner" or "inside", and @code{a}, which means "around".

@itemize
@item
@code{ci(} change the contents inside the current pair of parentheses
@item
@code{ci[} change the contents inside the current pair of square brackets
@item
@code{da'} delete a single-quoted string, including the surrounding single quotes
@end itemize

@node Development - Putting it all together, Development - Learning Vim, Development - Vim's interface is a programming language, Development - Text editing and Vim
@unnumberedsubsec Putting it all together
@cindex Putting it all together

Here is a broken @uref{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fizz_buzz,fizz buzz} implementation:

@example
def fizz_buzz(limit):
    for i in range(limit):
        if i % 3 == 0:
            print("fizz", end="")
        if i % 5 == 0:
            print("fizz", end="")
        if i % 3 and i % 5:
            print(i, end="")
        print()

def main():
    fizz_buzz(20)
@end example

We use the following sequence of commands to fix the issues, beginning in Normal mode:

@itemize
@item
Main is never called
@itemize
@item
@code{G} to jump to the end of the file
@item
@code{o} to @strong{o}pen a new line below
@item
Type in @code{if __name__ == "__main__": main()}
@itemize
@item
If your editor has Python language support, it might do some auto-indentation for you in Insert mode
@end itemize

@item
@code{<ESC>} to go back to Normal mode
@end itemize

@item
Starts at 0 instead of 1
@itemize
@item
@code{/} followed by @code{range} and @code{<CR>} to search for "range"
@item
@code{ww} to move forward two @strong{w}ords (you could also use @code{2w}, but in practice, for small counts it's common to repeat the key instead of using the count functionality)
@item
@code{i} to switch to @strong{i}nsert mode, and add @code{1,}
@item
@code{<ESC>} to go back to Normal mode
@item
@code{e} to jump to the @strong{e}nd of the next word
@item
@code{a} to start @strong{a}ppending text, and add @code{+ 1}
@item
@code{<ESC>} to go back to Normal mode
@end itemize

@item
Prints "fizz" for multiples of 5
@itemize
@item
@code{:6<CR>} to go to line 6
@item
@code{ci"} to @strong{c}hange @strong{i}nside the '@strong{"}', change to @code{"buzz"}
@item
@code{<ESC>} to go back to Normal mode
@end itemize

@end itemize

@node Development - Learning Vim, , Development - Putting it all together, Development - Text editing and Vim
@unnumberedsubsec Learning Vim
@cindex Learning Vim

The best way to learn Vim is to learn the fundamentals (what we've covered so far) and then just enable Vim mode in all your software and start using it in practice. Avoid the temptation to use the mouse or the arrow keys; in some editors, you can unbind the arrow keys to force yourself to build good habits.

