@node Development - Code intelligence and language servers, Development - AI-powered development, Development - Text editing and Vim, Development
@unnumberedsec Code intelligence and language servers
@cindex Code intelligence and language servers

IDEs generally offer language-specific support that requires semantic understanding of the code through IDE extensions that connect to @emph{language servers} that implement @uref{https://microsoft.github.io/language-server-protocol/,Language Server Protocol}. For example, the @uref{https://marketplace.visualstudio.com/items?itemName=ms-python.python,Python extension for VS Code} relies on @uref{https://marketplace.visualstudio.com/items?itemName=ms-python.vscode-pylance,Pylance}, and the @uref{https://marketplace.visualstudio.com/items?itemName=golang.go,Go extension for VS Code} relies on the first-party @uref{https://go.dev/gopls/,gopls}. By installing the extension and language server for the languages you work with, you can enable many language-specific features in your IDE, such as:

@itemize
@item
@strong{Code completion.} Better autocomplete and autosuggest, such as being able to see an object's fields and methods after typing @code{object.}.
@item
@strong{Inline documentation.} Seeing documentation on hover and autosuggest.
@item
@strong{Jump-to-definition.} Jumping from a use site to the definition, such as being able to go from a field reference @code{object.field} to the definition of the field.
@item
@strong{Find references.} The inverse of the above, find all sites where a particular item such as a field or type is referenced.
@item
@strong{Help with imports.} Organizing imports, removing unused imports, flagging missing imports.
@item
@strong{Code quality.} These tools can be used standalone, but this functionality is often provided by language servers as well. Code formatting auto-indents and auto-formats code, and type checkers and linters find errors in your code, as you type. We will cover this class of functionality in greater depth in the @ref{Quality,,lecture on code quality}.
@end itemize

@menu
* Development - Configuring language servers:: Configuring language servers
@end menu

@node Development - Configuring language servers, , Development - Code intelligence and language servers, Development - Code intelligence and language servers
@unnumberedsubsec Configuring language servers
@cindex Configuring language servers

For some languages, all you need to do is install the extension and language server, and you'll be all set. For others, to get the maximum benefit from the language server, you need to tell the IDE about your environment. For example, pointing VS Code to your @uref{https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/python/environments,Python environment} will enable the language server to see your installed packages. Environments are covered in more depth in our @ref{Shipping,,lecture on packaging and shipping code}.

Depending on the language, there might be some settings you can configure for your language server. For example, using the Python support in VS Code, you can disable static type checking for projects that don't make use of Python's optional type annotations.

@node Development - AI-powered development, Development - Extensions and other IDE functionality, Development - Code intelligence and language servers, Development
@unnumberedsec AI-powered development
@cindex AI-powered development

Since the introduction of @uref{https://github.com/features/copilot/ai-code-editor,GitHub Copilot} using OpenAI's @uref{https://openai.com/index/openai-codex/,Codex model} in mid 2021, @uref{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Large_language_model,LLMs} have become widely adopted in software engineering. There are three main form factors in use right now: autocomplete, inline chat, and coding agents.

@menu
* Development - Autocomplete:: Autocomplete
* Development - Inline chat:: Inline chat
* Development - Coding agents:: Coding agents
* Development - Recommended software:: Recommended software
@end menu

@node Development - Autocomplete, Development - Inline chat, Development - AI-powered development, Development - AI-powered development
@unnumberedsubsec Autocomplete
@cindex Autocomplete

AI-powered autocomplete has the same form factor as traditional autocomplete in your IDE, suggesting completions at your cursor position as you type. Sometimes, it's used as a passive feature that "just works". Beyond that, AI autocomplete is generally @uref{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prompt_engineering,prompted} using code comments.

For example, let's write a script to download the contents of these lecture notes and extract all the links. We can start with:

@example
import requests

def download_contents(url: str) -> str:
@end example

The model will autocomplete the body of the function:

@example
    response = requests.get(url)
    return response.text
@end example

We can further guide completions using comments. For example, if we start writing a function to extract all Markdown links, but it doesn't have a particularly descriptive name:

@example
def extract(contents: str) -> list[str]:
@end example

The model will autocomplete something like this:

@example
    lines = contents.splitlines()
    return [line for line in lines if line.strip()]
@end example

We can guide the completion through code comments:

@example
def extract(content: str) -> list[str]:
    # extract all Markdown links from the content
@end example

This time, the model gives a better completion:

@example
    import re
    pattern = r'\[.*?\]\((.*?)\)'
    return re.findall(pattern, content)
@end example

Here, we see one downside of this AI coding tool: it can only provide completions at the cursor. In this case, it would be better practice to put the @code{import re} at the module level, rather than inside the function.

The example above used a poorly-named function to demonstrate how code completion can be steered using comments; in practice, you'd want to write code with functions named more descriptively, like @code{extract_links}, and you'd want to write docstrings (and based on this, the model should generate a completion analogous to the one above).

For demonstration purposes, we can complete the script:

@example
print(extract(download_contents("https://raw.githubusercontent.com/missing-semester/missing-semester/refs/heads/master/_2026/development-environment.md")))
@end example

@node Development - Inline chat, Development - Coding agents, Development - Autocomplete, Development - AI-powered development
@unnumberedsubsec Inline chat
@cindex Inline chat

Inline chat lets you select a line or block and then directly prompt the AI model to propose an edit. In this interaction mode, the model can make changes to existing code (which differs from autocomplete, which only completes code beyond the cursor).

Continuing the example from above, suppose we decided not to use the third-party @code{requests} library. We could select the relevant three lines of code, invoke inline chat, and say something like:

@example
use built-in libraries instead
@end example

The model proposes:

@example
from urllib.request import urlopen

def download_contents(url: str) -> str:
    with urlopen(url) as response:
        return response.read().decode('utf-8')
@end example

@node Development - Coding agents, Development - Recommended software, Development - Inline chat, Development - AI-powered development
@unnumberedsubsec Coding agents
@cindex Coding agents

Coding agents are covered in depth in the @ref{Agents,,Agentic Coding} lecture.

@node Development - Recommended software, , Development - Coding agents, Development - AI-powered development
@unnumberedsubsec Recommended software
@cindex Recommended software

Some popular AI IDEs are @uref{https://code.visualstudio.com/,VS Code} with the @uref{https://github.com/features/copilot/ai-code-editor,GitHub Copilot} extension and @uref{https://cursor.com/,Cursor}. GitHub Copilot is currently available @uref{https://github.com/education/students,for free for students}, teachers, and maintainers of popular open source projects. This is a rapidly evolving space. Many of the leading products have roughly equivalent functionality.

@node Development - Extensions and other IDE functionality, Development - Exercises, Development - AI-powered development, Development
@unnumberedsec Extensions and other IDE functionality
@cindex Extensions and other IDE functionality

IDEs are powerful tools, made even more powerful by @emph{extensions}. We can't cover all of these features in a single lecture, but here we provide some pointers to a couple popular extensions. We encourage you to explore this space on your own; there are many lists of popular IDE extensions available online, such as @uref{https://vimawesome.com/,Vim Awesome} for Vim plugins and @uref{https://marketplace.visualstudio.com/search?target=VSCode&category=All%20categories&sortBy=Installs,VS Code extensions sorted by popularity}.

@itemize
@item
@uref{https://containers.dev/,Development containers}: supported by popular IDEs (e.g., @uref{https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/devcontainers/containers,supported by VS Code}), dev containers let you use a container to run development tools. This can be helpful for portability or isolation. The @ref{Shipping,,lecture on packaging and shipping code} covers containers in more depth.
@item
Remote development: do development on a remote machine using SSH (e.g., with the @uref{https://marketplace.visualstudio.com/items?itemName=ms-vscode-remote.remote-ssh,Remote SSH plugin for VS Code}). This can be handy, for example, if you want to develop and run code on a beefy GPU machine in the cloud.
@item
Collaborative editing: edit the same file, Google Docs style (e.g., with the @uref{https://marketplace.visualstudio.com/items?itemName=MS-vsliveshare.vsliveshare,Live Share plugin for VS Code}).
@end itemize

@node Development - Exercises, , Development - Extensions and other IDE functionality, Development
@unnumberedsec Exercises
@cindex Exercises

@enumerate
@item
Enable Vim mode in all the software you use that supports it, such as your editor and your shell, and use Vim mode for all your text editing for the next month. Whenever something seems inefficient, or when you think "there must be a better way", try Googling it, there probably is a better way.
@item
Complete a challenge from @uref{https://www.vimgolf.com/,VimGolf}.
@item
Configure an IDE extension and language server for a project that you're working on. Ensure that all the expected functionality, such as jump-to-definition for library dependencies, works as expected. If you don't have code that you can use for this exercise, you can use some open-source project from GitHub (such as @uref{https://github.com/spf13/cobra,this one}).
@item
Browse a list of IDE extensions and install one that seems useful to you.
@end enumerate

