@heading Study guide (added for this edition)

Use evidence to explain a failure or a performance limit. This lecture moves from logs and breakpoints to execution recording, operating system tracing, memory checks, and resource measurements. Select a tool for the question you need to answer. Then reproduce the result before you change the program.

@subheading Learning objectives
@itemize @bullet
@item
Choose logs, a debugger, or a system call trace to inspect program behavior.
@item
Use a recorded execution to trace corrupted state back to its cause.
@item
Distinguish elapsed time, CPU time, memory use, and sampled call stacks.
@item
Measure a representative workload before and after an optimization.
@end itemize

@subheading Reading notes
@itemize @bullet
@item
The merge sort, memory corruption, and use-after-free exercises contain deliberate defects. Keep these defects until you use the specified tool to find them.
@item
Replace placeholders such as <PID> with actual values. Angle brackets in a command are shell syntax, not part of a process identifier.
@item
A flame graph groups stack samples. Its width shows the amount of sampled work; its horizontal position does not show when that work occurred.
@item
Tracing tools have different operating system, hardware, and permission requirements. Read the linked tool documentation when an example cannot run on your system.
@end itemize

@heading Original lecture notes

A golden rule in programming is that code does not do what you expect it to do, but what you tell it to do. Bridging that gap can sometimes be a quite difficult feat. In this lecture we are going to cover useful techniques for dealing with buggy and resource hungry code: debugging and profiling.

