@node Debugging - Printf Debugging and Logging, Debugging - Debuggers, Debugging - Debugging, Debugging - Debugging
@unnumberedsubsec Printf Debugging and Logging
@cindex Printf Debugging and Logging

@quotation
"The most effective debugging tool is still careful thought, coupled with judiciously placed print statements" --- Brian Kernighan, @emph{Unix for Beginners}.
@end quotation
A first approach to debug a program is to add print statements around where you have detected the problem, and keep iterating until you have extracted enough information to understand what is responsible for the issue.

A second approach is to use logging in your program, instead of ad hoc print statements. Logging is essentially "printing with more care", and is usually done through a logging framework that includes built-in support for things like:

@itemize
@item
the ability to direct the logs (or subsets of the logs) to other output locations;
@item
setting severity levels (such as INFO, DEBUG, WARN, ERROR, etc.) and allow you to filter the output according to those; and
@item
support for structured logging of data related to the log entries, which can then be extracted more easily after the fact.
@end itemize

Logging statements you'll also usually proactively put in while programming so that the data you need to debug may already be there! And indeed, once you've found and fixed a problem using print statements, it's often worthwhile to convert those prints into proper log statements before removing them. This way, if similar bugs occur in the future, you'll already have the diagnostic information you need without modifying the code.

@quotation
@strong{Third-party logs}: Many programs support the @code{-v} or @code{--verbose} flag to print more information when they run. This can be useful for discovering why a given command fails. Some even allow repeating the flag for more details. When debugging issues with services (databases, web servers, etc.), check their logs---often in @code{/var/log/} on Linux. Use @code{journalctl -u <service>} to view logs for systemd services. For third-party libraries, check if they support debug logging via environment variables or configuration.
@end quotation

@node Debugging - Debuggers, Debugging - System Call Tracing, Debugging - Printf Debugging and Logging, Debugging - Debugging
@unnumberedsubsec Debuggers
@cindex Debuggers

Print debugging works well when you know what to print and can easily modify and re-run your code. Debuggers become valuable when you're not sure what information you need, when the bug only manifests in hard-to-reproduce conditions, or when modifying and restarting the program is expensive (long startup times, complex state to recreate, etc.).

Debuggers are programs that let you interact with the execution of a program as it happens, allowing you to:

@itemize
@item
Halt execution when it reaches a certain line.
@item
Step through one instruction at a time.
@item
Inspect values of variables after a crash.
@item
Conditionally halt execution when a given condition is met.
@item
And many more advanced features.
@end itemize

Most programming languages support (or come with) some form of debugger. The most versatile are @strong{general-purpose debuggers} like @uref{https://www.gnu.org/software/gdb/,gdb} (GNU Debugger) and @uref{https://lldb.llvm.org/,lldb} (LLVM Debugger), which can debug any native binary. Many languages also have @strong{language-specific debuggers} that integrate more tightly with the runtime (like Python's pdb or Java's jdb).

@code{gdb} is the de-facto standard debugger for C, C++, Rust, and other compiled languages. It lets you probe pretty much any process and get its current machine state: registers, stack, program counter, and more.

Some useful GDB commands:

@itemize
@item
@code{run} - Start the program
@item
@code{b @{function@}} or @code{b @{file@}:@{line@}} - Set a breakpoint
@item
@code{c} - Continue execution
@item
@code{step} / @code{next} / @code{finish} - Step in / step over / step out
@item
@code{p @{variable@}} - Print value of variable
@item
@code{bt} - Show backtrace (call stack)
@item
@code{watch @{expression@}} - Break when the value changes
@end itemize

@quotation
Consider using GDB's TUI mode (@code{gdb -tui} or press @code{Ctrl-x a} inside GDB) for a split-screen view showing source code alongside the command prompt.
@end quotation

@menu
* Debugging - Record-Replay Debugging:: Record-Replay Debugging
@end menu

@node Debugging - Record-Replay Debugging, , Debugging - Debuggers, Debugging - Debuggers
@unnumberedsubsubsec Record-Replay Debugging
@cindex Record-Replay Debugging

Some of the most frustrating bugs are @emph{Heisenbugs}: bugs that seem to disappear or change behavior when you try to observe them. Race conditions, timing-dependent bugs, and issues that only appear under certain system conditions fall into this category. Traditional debugging is often useless here because running the program again produces different behavior (e.g., print statements may slow down the code sufficiently that the race no longer happens).

@strong{Record-replay debugging} solves this by recording a program's execution and allowing you to replay it deterministically as many times as you need. Even better, you can @emph{reverse} through the execution to find exactly where things went wrong.

@uref{https://rr-project.org/,rr} is a powerful tool for Linux that records program execution and allows deterministic replay with full debugging capabilities. It works with GDB, so you already know the interface.

Basic usage:

@example
# Record a program execution
rr record ./my_program

# Replay the recording (opens GDB)
rr replay
@end example

The magic happens during replay. Because the execution is deterministic, you can use @strong{reverse debugging} commands:

@itemize
@item
@code{reverse-continue} (@code{rc}) - Run backwards until hitting a breakpoint
@item
@code{reverse-step} (@code{rs}) - Step backwards one line
@item
@code{reverse-next} (@code{rn}) - Step backwards, skipping function calls
@item
@code{reverse-finish} - Run backwards until entering the current function
@end itemize

This is incredibly powerful for debugging. Say you have a crash---instead of guessing where the bug is and setting breakpoints, you can:

@enumerate
@item
Run to the crash
@item
Inspect the corrupted state
@item
Set a watchpoint on the corrupted variable
@item
@code{reverse-continue} to find exactly where it was corrupted
@end enumerate

@strong{When to use rr:}

@itemize
@item
Flaky tests that fail intermittently
@item
Race conditions and threading bugs
@item
Crashes that are hard to reproduce
@item
Any bug where you wish you could "go back in time"
@end itemize

@quotation
Note: rr only works on Linux and requires hardware performance counters. It doesn't work in VMs that don't expose these counters, such as on most AWS EC2 instances, and it doesn't support GPU access. For macOS, check out @uref{https://warpspeed.dev/,Warpspeed}.
@end quotation
@quotation
@strong{rr and concurrency}: Because rr records execution deterministically, it serializes thread scheduling. This means some race conditions may not manifest under rr if they depend on specific timing. rr is still useful for debugging races---once you capture a failing run, you can replay it reliably---but you may need multiple recording attempts to catch an intermittent bug. For bugs that don't involve concurrency, rr shines brightest: you can always reproduce the exact execution and use reverse debugging to hunt down corruption.
@end quotation

@node Debugging - System Call Tracing, Debugging - Memory Debugging, Debugging - Debuggers, Debugging - Debugging
@unnumberedsubsec System Call Tracing
@cindex System Call Tracing

Sometimes you need to understand how your program interacts with the operating system. Programs make @uref{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/System_call,system calls} to request services from the kernel---opening files, allocating memory, creating processes, and more. Tracing these calls can reveal why a program is hanging, what files it's trying to access, or where it's spending time waiting.

@menu
* Debugging - strace Linux and dtruss macOS:: strace (Linux) and dtruss (macOS)
* Debugging - bpftrace and eBPF:: bpftrace and eBPF
* Debugging - Network Debugging:: Network Debugging
@end menu

@node Debugging - strace Linux and dtruss macOS, Debugging - bpftrace and eBPF, Debugging - System Call Tracing, Debugging - System Call Tracing
@unnumberedsubsubsec strace (Linux) and dtruss (macOS)
@cindex strace (Linux) and dtruss (macOS)

@uref{https://www.man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/strace.1.html,strace} lets you observe every system call a program makes:

@example
# Trace all system calls
strace ./my_program

# Trace only file-related calls
strace -e trace=file ./my_program

# Follow child processes (important for programs that start other programs)
strace -f ./my_program

# Trace a running process
strace -p <PID>

# Show timing information
strace -T ./my_program
@end example

@quotation
On macOS and BSD, use @uref{https://www.manpagez.com/man/1/dtruss/,dtruss} (which wraps @code{dtrace}) for similar functionality:
@end quotation
@quotation
For deeper dives into @code{strace}, check out Julia Evans' excellent @uref{https://jvns.ca/strace-zine-unfolded.pdf,strace zine}.
@end quotation

@node Debugging - bpftrace and eBPF, Debugging - Network Debugging, Debugging - strace Linux and dtruss macOS, Debugging - System Call Tracing
@unnumberedsubsubsec bpftrace and eBPF
@cindex bpftrace and eBPF

@uref{https://ebpf.io/,eBPF} (extended Berkeley Packet Filter) is a powerful Linux technology that allows running sandboxed programs in the kernel. @uref{https://github.com/iovisor/bpftrace,bpftrace} provides a high-level syntax for writing eBPF programs. These are arbitrary programs running in the kernel, and thus have huge expressive power (though also a somewhat clumsy awk-like syntax). The most common use-case for them is to investigate what system calls are being invoked, including aggregations (like counts or latency statistics) or introspecting (or even filtering on) system call arguments.

@example
# Trace file opens system-wide (prints immediately)
sudo bpftrace -e 'tracepoint:syscalls:sys_enter_openat @{ printf("%s %s\n", comm, str(args->filename)); @}'

# Count system calls by name (prints summary on Ctrl-C)
sudo bpftrace -e 'tracepoint:syscalls:sys_enter_* @{ @@[probe] = count(); @}'
@end example

However, you can also write eBPF programs directly in C using a toolchain like @uref{https://github.com/iovisor/bcc,bcc}, which also ships with @uref{https://www.brendangregg.com/blog/2015-09-22/bcc-linux-4.3-tracing.html,many handy tools} like @code{biosnoop} for printing latency distributions for disk operations or @code{opensnoop} for printing all open files.

Where @code{strace} is useful because it's easy to "just get up and running", @code{bpftrace} is what you should reach for when you need lower overhead, want to trace through kernel functions, need to do any kind of aggregation, etc. Note that @code{bpftrace} has to run as @code{root} though, and that it generally monitors the entire kernel, not just a particular process. To target a specific program, you can filter by command name or PID:

@example
# Filter by command name (prints summary on Ctrl-C)
sudo bpftrace -e 'tracepoint:syscalls:sys_enter_* /comm == "bash"/ @{ @@[probe] = count(); @}'

# Trace a specific command from startup using -c (cpid = child PID)
sudo bpftrace -e 'tracepoint:syscalls:sys_enter_* /pid == cpid/ @{ @@[probe] = count(); @}' -c 'ls -la'
@end example

The @code{-c} flag runs the specified command and sets @code{cpid} to its PID, which is useful for tracing a program from the moment it starts. When the traced command exits, bpftrace prints the aggregated results.

@node Debugging - Network Debugging, , Debugging - bpftrace and eBPF, Debugging - System Call Tracing
@unnumberedsubsubsec Network Debugging
@cindex Network Debugging

For network issues, @uref{https://www.man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/tcpdump.1.html,tcpdump} and @uref{https://www.wireshark.org/,Wireshark} let you capture and analyze network packets:

@example
# Capture packets on port 80
sudo tcpdump -i any port 80

# Capture and save to file for Wireshark analysis
sudo tcpdump -i any -w capture.pcap
@end example

For HTTPS traffic, the encryption makes tcpdump less useful. Tools like @uref{https://mitmproxy.org/,mitmproxy} can act as an intercepting proxy to inspect encrypted traffic. Browser developer tools (Network tab) are often the easiest way to debug HTTPS requests from web applications---they show decrypted request/response data, headers, and timing.

@node Debugging - Memory Debugging, Debugging - AI for Debugging, Debugging - System Call Tracing, Debugging - Debugging
@unnumberedsubsec Memory Debugging
@cindex Memory Debugging

Memory bugs---buffer overflows, use-after-free, memory leaks---are among the most dangerous and difficult to debug. They often don't crash immediately but corrupt memory in ways that cause problems much later.

@menu
* Debugging - Sanitizers:: Sanitizers
* Debugging - Valgrind When You Can't Recompile:: Valgrind: When You Can't Recompile
@end menu

@node Debugging - Sanitizers, Debugging - Valgrind When You Can't Recompile, Debugging - Memory Debugging, Debugging - Memory Debugging
@unnumberedsubsubsec Sanitizers
@cindex Sanitizers

One approach to finding memory bugs is to use @strong{sanitizers}, which are compiler features that instrument your code to detect errors at runtime. For example, the widely used @strong{AddressSanitizer (ASan)} detects:

@itemize
@item
Buffer overflows (stack, heap, and global)
@item
Use-after-free
@item
Use-after-return
@item
Memory leaks
@end itemize

@example
# Compile with AddressSanitizer
gcc -fsanitize=address -g program.c -o program
./program
@end example

There are a variety of useful sanitizers:

@itemize
@item
@strong{ThreadSanitizer (TSan)}: Detects data races in multithreaded code (@code{-fsanitize=thread})
@item
@strong{MemorySanitizer (MSan)}: Detects reads of uninitialized memory (@code{-fsanitize=memory})
@item
@strong{UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer (UBSan)}: Detects undefined behavior like integer overflow (@code{-fsanitize=undefined})
@end itemize

Sanitizers require recompilation but are fast enough to use in CI pipelines and during regular development.

@node Debugging - Valgrind When You Can't Recompile, , Debugging - Sanitizers, Debugging - Memory Debugging
@unnumberedsubsubsec Valgrind: When You Can't Recompile
@cindex Valgrind: When You Can't Recompile

@uref{https://valgrind.org/,Valgrind} instead runs your program in something akin to a virtual machine to detect memory errors. It's slower than sanitizers but doesn't require recompilation:

@example
valgrind --leak-check=full ./my_program
@end example

Use Valgrind when:

@itemize
@item
You don't have source code
@item
You can't recompile (third-party libraries)
@item
You need specific tools not available as sanitizers
@end itemize

Valgrind is actually a really powerful controlled execution environment, and we'll see more of it later when we get to profiling!

@node Debugging - AI for Debugging, , Debugging - Memory Debugging, Debugging - Debugging
@unnumberedsubsec AI for Debugging
@cindex AI for Debugging

Large language models have become surprisingly useful debugging assistants. They excel at certain debugging tasks that complement traditional tools.

@strong{Where LLMs shine:}

@itemize
@item
@strong{Explaining cryptic error messages}: Compiler errors, especially from C++ templates or Rust's borrow checker, can be notoriously cryptic. LLMs can translate them into plain English and suggest fixes.

@item
@strong{Traversing language and abstraction boundaries}: If you're debugging a problem that spans multiple languages (say, a bug in a C library that manifests through a Python binding), LLMs can help navigate the different layers. They're particularly good at understanding FFI boundaries, build system issues, and cross-language debugging (e.g., my program errors, but I believe it is because of a bug in one of my dependencies).

@item
@strong{Correlating symptoms with root causes}: "My program works fine but uses 10x more memory than expected" is the kind of vague symptom that LLMs can help investigate, suggesting likely causes and what to look for.

@item
@strong{Analyzing crash dumps and stack traces}: Paste a stack trace and ask what might have caused it.

@end itemize

@quotation
@b{Note on debug symbols}: For meaningful stack traces and debugging, ensure your binaries (and any linked libraries) are compiled with debug symbols (@code{-g} flag). Debug information is typically stored in DWARF format. Additionally, compiling with frame pointers (@code{-fno-omit-frame-pointer}) makes stack traces more reliable, especially for profiling tools. Without these, stack traces may show only memory addresses or be incomplete. This matters more for natively compiled programs (C++, Rust) than Python or Java.
@end quotation
@strong{Limitations to keep in mind:}

@itemize
@item
LLMs can hallucinate plausible-sounding but wrong explanations
@item
They may suggest fixes that mask the bug rather than fix it
@item
Always verify suggestions with actual debugging tools
@item
They work best as a complement to, not replacement for, understanding your code
@end itemize

@quotation
This is distinct from the @ref{Development - AI-powered development,,general AI coding capabilities} covered in the Development Environment lecture. Here we're specifically talking about using LLMs as a debugging aid.
@end quotation

@node Debugging - Profiling, Debugging - Exercises, Debugging - Debugging, Debugging
@unnumberedsec Profiling
@cindex Profiling

Even if your code functionally behaves as you would expect, that might not be good enough if it takes all your CPU or memory in the process. Algorithms classes often teach big @emph{O} notation but not how to find hot spots in your programs. Since @uref{https://wiki.c2.com/?PrematureOptimization,premature optimization is the root of all evil}, you should learn about profilers and monitoring tools. They will help you understand which parts of your program are taking most of the time and/or resources so you can focus on optimizing those parts.

@menu
* Debugging - Timing:: Timing
* Debugging - Resource Monitoring:: Resource Monitoring
* Debugging - Visualizing Performance Data:: Visualizing Performance Data
* Debugging - CPU Profilers:: CPU Profilers
* Debugging - Memory Profilers:: Memory Profilers
* Debugging - Benchmarking:: Benchmarking
@end menu

@node Debugging - Timing, Debugging - Resource Monitoring, Debugging - Profiling, Debugging - Profiling
@unnumberedsubsec Timing
@cindex Timing

The simplest way to measure performance is to time things. In many scenarios it can be enough to just print the time it took your code between two points.

However, wall clock time can be misleading since your computer might be running other processes at the same time or waiting for events to happen. The @code{time} command distinguishes between @emph{Real}, @emph{User}, and @emph{Sys} time:

@itemize
@item
@strong{Real} - Wall clock time from start to finish, including time spent waiting
@item
@strong{User} - Time spent in the CPU running user code
@item
@strong{Sys} - Time spent in the CPU running kernel code
@end itemize

@example
$ time curl https://missing.csail.mit.edu &> /dev/null
real    0m0.272s
user    0m0.079s
sys     0m0.028s
@end example

Here the request took nearly 300 milliseconds (real time) but only 107ms of CPU time (user + sys). The rest was waiting for the network.

@node Debugging - Resource Monitoring, Debugging - Visualizing Performance Data, Debugging - Timing, Debugging - Profiling
@unnumberedsubsec Resource Monitoring
@cindex Resource Monitoring

Sometimes the first step towards analyzing the performance of your program is to understand what its actual resource consumption is. Programs often run slowly when they are resource constrained.

@itemize
@item
@strong{General Monitoring}: @uref{https://htop.dev/,htop} is an improved version of @code{top} that presents various statistics for currently running processes. Useful keybinds: @code{<F6>} to sort processes, @code{t} to show tree hierarchy, @code{h} to toggle threads. There's also @uref{https://github.com/aristocratos/btop,btop} which monitors @emph{way} more things.

@item
@strong{I/O Operations}: @uref{https://www.man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/iotop.8.html,iotop} displays live I/O usage information.

@item
@strong{Memory Usage}: @uref{https://www.man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/free.1.html,free} displays total free and used memory.

@item
@strong{Open Files}: @uref{https://www.man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/lsof.8.html,lsof} lists file information about files opened by processes. Useful for checking which process has opened a specific file.

@item
@strong{Network Connections}: @uref{https://www.man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/ss.8.html,ss} lets you monitor network connections. A common use case is figuring out what process is using a given port: @code{ss -tlnp | grep :8080}.

@item
@strong{Network Usage}: @uref{https://github.com/raboof/nethogs,nethogs} and @uref{https://pdw.ex-parrot.com/iftop/,iftop} are good interactive CLI tools for monitoring network usage per process.

@end itemize

@node Debugging - Visualizing Performance Data, Debugging - CPU Profilers, Debugging - Resource Monitoring, Debugging - Profiling
@unnumberedsubsec Visualizing Performance Data
@cindex Visualizing Performance Data

Humans spot patterns in graphs much faster than in tables of numbers. When analyzing performance, plotting your data often reveals trends, spikes, and anomalies that would be invisible in raw numbers.

@strong{Making data plottable}: When adding print or log statements for debugging, consider formatting the output so it can be easily graphed later. A simple timestamp and value in CSV format (@code{1705012345,42.5}) is much easier to plot than a prose sentence. JSON-structured logs can also be parsed and plotted with minimal effort. In other words, log your data @uref{https://vita.had.co.nz/papers/tidy-data.pdf,in a tidy way}.

@strong{Quick plotting with gnuplot}: For simple command-line plotting, @uref{http://www.gnuplot.info/,gnuplot} can generate graphs directly from data files:

@example
# Plot a simple CSV with timestamp,value
gnuplot -e "set datafile separator ','; plot 'latency.csv' using 1:2 with lines"
@end example

@strong{Iterative exploration with matplotlib and ggplot2}: For deeper analysis, Python's @uref{https://matplotlib.org/,matplotlib} and R's @uref{https://ggplot2.tidyverse.org/,ggplot2} enable iterative exploration. Unlike one-off plotting, these tools let you quickly slice and transform data to investigate hypotheses. ggplot2's facet plots are particularly powerful---you can split a single dataset across multiple subplots by category (e.g., faceting request latency by endpoint or time-of-day) to tease out patterns that would otherwise be hidden.

@strong{Example use cases:}

@itemize
@item
Plotting request latency over time reveals periodic slowdowns (garbage collection, cron jobs, traffic patterns) that raw percentiles obscure
@item
Visualizing insert times for a growing data structure can expose algorithmic complexity issues---a plot of vector insertions will show characteristic spikes when the backing array doubles in size
@item
Faceting metrics by different dimensions (request type, user cohort, server) often reveals that a "system-wide" problem is actually isolated to one category
@end itemize

@node Debugging - CPU Profilers, Debugging - Memory Profilers, Debugging - Visualizing Performance Data, Debugging - Profiling
@unnumberedsubsec CPU Profilers
@cindex CPU Profilers

Most of the time when people refer to @emph{profilers} they mean @emph{CPU profilers}. There are two main types:

@itemize
@item
@strong{Tracing profilers} keep a record of every function call your program makes
@item
@strong{Sampling profilers} probe your program periodically (commonly every millisecond) and record the program's stack
@end itemize

Sampling profilers have lower overhead and are generally preferred for production use.

@menu
* Debugging - perf the sampling profiler:: perf: the sampling profiler
* Debugging - Valgrind's Callgrind the tracing profiler:: Valgrind's Callgrind: the tracing profiler
@end menu

@node Debugging - perf the sampling profiler, Debugging - Valgrind's Callgrind the tracing profiler, Debugging - CPU Profilers, Debugging - CPU Profilers
@unnumberedsubsubsec perf: the sampling profiler
@cindex perf: the sampling profiler

@uref{https://www.man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/perf.1.html,perf} is the standard Linux profiler. It can profile any program without recompilation:

@code{perf stat} gives you a quick overview of where time is spent:

@example
$ perf stat ./slow_program

 Performance counter stats for './slow_program':

         3,210.45 msec task-clock                #    0.998 CPUs utilized
               12      context-switches          #    3.738 /sec
                0      cpu-migrations            #    0.000 /sec
              156      page-faults               #   48.587 /sec
   12,345,678,901      cycles                    #    3.845 GHz
    9,876,543,210      instructions              #    0.80  insn per cycle
    1,234,567,890      branches                  #  384.532 M/sec
       12,345,678      branch-misses             #    1.00% of all branches
@end example

Profiler output for real world programs will contain large amounts of information. Humans are visual creatures and are quite terrible at reading large amounts of numbers. @uref{https://www.brendangregg.com/flamegraphs.html,Flame graphs} are a visualization that makes profiling data much easier to understand.

A flame graph displays a hierarchy of function calls across the Y axis and time taken proportional to the X axis. They're interactive---you can click to zoom into specific parts of the program.

@uref{https://www.brendangregg.com/FlameGraphs/cpu-bash-flamegraph.svg,FlameGraph}

To generate a flame graph from @code{perf} data:

@example
# Record profile
perf record -g ./my_program

# Generate flame graph (requires flamegraph scripts)
perf script | stackcollapse-perf.pl | flamegraph.pl > flamegraph.svg
@end example

@quotation
Consider using @uref{https://www.speedscope.app/,Speedscope} for an interactive web-based flame graph viewer, or @uref{https://perfetto.dev/,Perfetto} for comprehensive system-level analysis.
@end quotation

@node Debugging - Valgrind's Callgrind the tracing profiler, , Debugging - perf the sampling profiler, Debugging - CPU Profilers
@unnumberedsubsubsec Valgrind's Callgrind: the tracing profiler
@cindex Valgrind's Callgrind: the tracing profiler

@uref{https://valgrind.org/docs/manual/cl-manual.html,callgrind} is a profiling tool that records the call history and instruction counts of your program. Unlike sampling profilers, it provides exact call counts and can show the relationship between callers and callees:

@example
# Run with callgrind
valgrind --tool=callgrind ./my_program

# Analyze with callgrind_annotate (text) or kcachegrind (GUI)
callgrind_annotate callgrind.out.<pid>
kcachegrind callgrind.out.<pid>
@end example

Callgrind is slower than sampling profilers but provides precise call counts and can optionally simulate cache behavior (with @code{--cache-sim=yes}) if you need that information.

@quotation
If you're using a particular language, there may be more specialized profilers. For example, Python has @uref{https://docs.python.org/3/library/profile.html,cProfile} and @uref{https://github.com/benfred/py-spy,py-spy}, Go has @uref{https://pkg.go.dev/cmd/pprof,go tool pprof}, and Rust has @uref{https://github.com/flamegraph-rs/flamegraph,cargo-flamegraph} (which actually works for any compiled program!).
@end quotation

@node Debugging - Memory Profilers, Debugging - Benchmarking, Debugging - CPU Profilers, Debugging - Profiling
@unnumberedsubsec Memory Profilers
@cindex Memory Profilers

Memory profilers help you understand how your program uses memory over time and find memory leaks.

@menu
* Debugging - Valgrind's Massif:: Valgrind's Massif
@end menu

@node Debugging - Valgrind's Massif, , Debugging - Memory Profilers, Debugging - Memory Profilers
@unnumberedsubsubsec Valgrind's Massif
@cindex Valgrind's Massif

@uref{https://valgrind.org/docs/manual/ms-manual.html,massif} profiles heap memory usage:

@example
valgrind --tool=massif ./my_program
ms_print massif.out.<pid>
@end example

This shows you heap usage over time, helping identify memory leaks and excessive allocation.

@quotation
For Python, @uref{https://pypi.org/project/memory-profiler/,memory-profiler} provides line-by-line memory usage information.
@end quotation

@node Debugging - Benchmarking, , Debugging - Memory Profilers, Debugging - Profiling
