@node Debugging - Debugging - more, Debugging - Profiling - more, Debugging - Exercises, Debugging - Exercises
@unnumberedsubsec Debugging
@cindex Debugging

@enumerate
@item
@strong{Debug a sorting algorithm}: The following pseudocode implements merge sort but contains a bug. Implement it in a language of your choice, then use a debugger (gdb, lldb, pdb, or your IDE's debugger) to find and fix the bug.

@example
function merge_sort(arr):
    if length(arr) <= 1:
        return arr
    mid = length(arr) / 2
    left = merge_sort(arr[0..mid])
    right = merge_sort(arr[mid..end])
    return merge(left, right)

function merge(left, right):
    result = []
    i = 0, j = 0
    while i < length(left) AND j < length(right):
        if left[i] <= right[j]:
            append result, left[i]
            i = i + 1
        else:
            append result, right[i]
            j = j + 1
    append remaining elements from left and right
    return result
@end example

Test vector: @code{merge_sort([3, 1, 4, 1, 5, 9, 2, 6])} should return @code{[1, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 9]}. Use breakpoints and step through the merge function to find where the incorrect element is being selected.

@item
Install @uref{https://rr-project.org/,rr} and use reverse debugging to find a corruption bug. Save this program as @code{corruption.c}:

@example
#include <stdio.h>

typedef struct @{
    int id;
    int scores[3];
@} Student;

Student students[2];

void init() @{
    students[0].id = 1001;
    students[0].scores[0] = 85;
    students[0].scores[1] = 92;
    students[0].scores[2] = 78;

    students[1].id = 1002;
    students[1].scores[0] = 90;
    students[1].scores[1] = 88;
    students[1].scores[2] = 95;
@}

void curve_scores(int student_idx, int curve) @{
    for (int i = 0; i < 4; i++) @{
        students[student_idx].scores[i] += curve;
    @}
@}

int main() @{
    init();
    printf("=== Initial state ===\n");
    printf("Student 0: id=%d\n", students[0].id);
    printf("Student 1: id=%d\n", students[1].id);

    curve_scores(0, 5);

    printf("\n=== After curving ===\n");
    printf("Student 0: id=%d\n", students[0].id);
    printf("Student 1: id=%d\n", students[1].id);

    if (students[1].id != 1002) @{
        printf("\nERROR: Student 1's ID was corrupted! Expected 1002, got %d\n",
               students[1].id);
        return 1;
    @}
    return 0;
@}
@end example

Compile with @code{gcc -g corruption.c -o corruption} and run it. Student 1's ID gets corrupted, but the corruption happens in a function that only touches student 0. Use @code{rr record ./corruption} and @code{rr replay} to find the culprit. Set a watchpoint on @code{students[1].id} and use @code{reverse-continue} after the corruption to find exactly which line of code overwrote it.

@item
Debug a memory error with AddressSanitizer. Save this as @code{uaf.c}:

@example
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <stdio.h>

int main() @{
    char *greeting = malloc(32);
    strcpy(greeting, "Hello, world!");
    printf("%s\n", greeting);

    free(greeting);

    greeting[0] = 'J';
    printf("%s\n", greeting);

    return 0;
@}
@end example

First compile and run without sanitizers: @code{gcc uaf.c -o uaf && ./uaf}. It may appear to work. Now compile with AddressSanitizer: @code{gcc -fsanitize=address -g uaf.c -o uaf && ./uaf}. Read the error report. What bug does ASan find? Fix the issue it identifies.

@item
Use @code{strace} (Linux) or @code{dtruss} (macOS) to trace the system calls made by a command like @code{ls -l}. What system calls is it making? Try tracing a more complex program and see what files it opens.

@item
Use an LLM to help debug a cryptic error message. Try copying a compiler error (especially from C++ templates or Rust) and asking for an explanation and fix. Try putting some of the output from @code{strace} or the address sanitizer into it.

@end enumerate

@node Debugging - Profiling - more, , Debugging - Debugging - more, Debugging - Exercises
@unnumberedsubsec Profiling
@cindex Profiling

@enumerate
@item
Use @code{perf stat} to get basic performance statistics for a program of your choice. What do the different counters mean?

@item
Profile with @code{perf record}. Save this as @code{slow.c}:

@example
#include <math.h>
#include <stdio.h>

double slow_computation(int n) @{
    double result = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) @{
        for (int j = 0; j < 1000; j++) @{
            result += sin(i * j) * cos(i + j);
        @}
    @}
    return result;
@}

int main() @{
    double r = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++) @{
        r += slow_computation(1000);
    @}
    printf("Result: %f\n", r);
    return 0;
@}
@end example

Compile with debug symbols: @code{gcc -g -O2 slow.c -o slow -lm}. Run @code{perf record -g ./slow}, then @code{perf report} to see where time is spent. Try generating a flame graph using the flamegraph scripts.

@item
Use @code{hyperfine} to benchmark two different implementations of the same task (e.g., @code{find} vs @code{fd}, @code{grep} vs @code{ripgrep}, or two versions of your own code).

@item
Use @code{htop} to monitor your system while running a resource-intensive program. Try using @code{taskset} to limit which CPUs a process can use: @code{taskset --cpu-list 0,2 stress -c 3}. Why doesn't @code{stress} use three CPUs?

@item
A common issue is that a port you want to listen on is already taken by another process. Learn how to discover that process: First execute @code{python -m http.server 4444} to start a minimal web server on port 4444. On a separate terminal run @code{ss -tlnp | grep 4444} to find the process. Terminate it with @code{kill <PID>}.

@end enumerate

