@node Git - Exercises, , Git - Resources, Git
@unnumberedsec Exercises
@cindex Exercises

@enumerate
@item
If you don't have any past experience with Git, either try reading the first couple chapters of @uref{https://git-scm.com/book/en/v2,Pro Git} or go through a tutorial like @uref{https://learngitbranching.js.org/,Learn Git Branching}. As you're working through it, relate Git commands to the data model.
@item
Clone the @uref{https://github.com/missing-semester/missing-semester,repository for the class website}.
@enumerate
@item
Explore the version history by visualizing it as a graph.
@item
Who was the last person to modify @code{README.md}? (Hint: use @code{git log} with an argument).
@item
What was the commit message associated with the last modification to the @code{collections:} line of @code{_config.yml}? (Hint: use @code{git blame} and @code{git show}).
@end enumerate

@item
One common mistake when learning Git is to commit large files that should not be managed by Git or adding sensitive information. Try adding a file to a repository, making some commits and then deleting that file from @emph{history} (not just the latest commit). You may want to look at @uref{https://help.github.com/articles/removing-sensitive-data-from-a-repository/,this}.
@item
Clone some repository from GitHub, and modify one of its existing files. What happens when you do @code{git stash}? What do you see when running @code{git log --all --oneline}? Run @code{git stash pop} to undo what you did with @code{git stash}. In what scenario might this be useful?
@item
Like many command line tools, Git provides a configuration file (or dotfile) called @code{~/.gitconfig}. Create an alias in @code{~/.gitconfig} so that when you run @code{git graph}, you get the output of @code{git log --all --graph --decorate --oneline}. You can do this by directly @uref{https://git-scm.com/docs/git-config#Documentation/git-config.txt-alias,editing} the @code{~/.gitconfig} file, or you can use the @code{git config} command to add the alias. Information about git aliases can be found @uref{https://git-scm.com/book/en/v2/Git-Basics-Git-Aliases,here}.
@item
You can define global ignore patterns in @code{~/.gitignore_global} after running @code{git config --global core.excludesfile ~/.gitignore_global}. This sets the location of the global ignore file that Git will use, but you still need to manually create the file at that path. Set up your global gitignore file to ignore OS-specific or editor-specific temporary files, like @code{.DS_Store}.
@item
Fork the @uref{https://github.com/missing-semester/missing-semester,repository for the class website}, find a typo or some other improvement you can make, and submit a pull request on GitHub (you may want to look at @uref{https://github.com/firstcontributions/first-contributions,this}). Please only submit PRs that are useful (don't spam us, please!). If you can't find an improvement to make, you can skip this exercise.
@item
Practice resolving merge conflicts by simulating a collaborative scenario:
@enumerate
@item
Create a new repository with @code{git init} and create a file called @code{recipe.txt} with a few lines (e.g., a simple recipe).
@item
Commit it, then create two branches: @code{git branch salty} and @code{git branch sweet}.
@item
In the @code{salty} branch, modify a line (e.g., change "1 cup sugar" to "1 cup salt") and commit.
@item
In the @code{sweet} branch, modify the same line differently (e.g., change "1 cup sugar" to "2 cups sugar") and commit.
@item
Now switch to @code{master} and try @code{git merge salty}, then @code{git merge sweet}. What happens? Look at the contents of @code{recipe.txt} - what do the @code{<<<<<<<}, @code{=======}, and @code{>>>>>>>} markers mean?
@item
Resolve the conflict by editing the file to keep the content you want, removing the conflict markers, and completing the merge with @code{git add} and @code{git commit} (or @code{git merge --continue}). Alternatively, try using @code{git mergetool} to resolve the conflict with a graphical or terminal-based merge tool.
@item
Use @code{git log --graph --oneline} to visualize the merge history you just created.
@end enumerate

@end enumerate

