@heading Study guide (added for this edition)

Shipping code requires an artifact and an explicit description of its environment. This lecture uses Python packages to explain dependencies, isolated environments, release versions, and reproducible builds. It then adds containers, configuration, supporting services, and package registries. Track what each step produces and what the next step needs.

@subheading Learning objectives
@itemize @bullet
@item
Separate source code, build artifacts, installed packages, and running services.
@item
Create a project environment and describe package metadata in pyproject.toml.
@item
Distinguish compatible dependency ranges from a locked application environment.
@item
Explain how images, containers, configuration, and Compose services fit together.
@end itemize

@subheading Reading notes
@itemize @bullet
@item
A fresh virtual environment does not contain requests. Install it in that environment before you run the lecture's import example. The displayed transcript omits this installation step.
@item
An image tag such as python:3.12 or uv:latest can change. The improved Dockerfile demonstrates build caching but does not pin every input or switch to a non-root user.
@item
Compose depends_on starts services in order. An application must still handle a dependency that is not ready to accept connections.
@item
The systemd example starts Compose at boot. Its oneshot unit does not itself restart failed application containers; configure the container restart policy for that behavior.
@item
Publishing examples include placeholder package URLs and version-specific output. Substitute your own artifact and registry details.
@end itemize

@heading Original lecture notes

Getting code to work as intended is hard; getting that same code to run on a machine different from your own is often harder.

Shipping code means taking the code you wrote and converting it into a usable form that someone else can run without your computer's exact setup. Shipping code takes many forms and depends on the choices of programming language, system libraries, and operating system, among many other factors. It also depends on what you are building: a software library, a command line tool, and a web service all have different requirements and deployment steps. Regardless, there is a common pattern between all these scenarios: we need to define what the deliverable is --- a.k.a. the @emph{artifact} --- and what assumptions it makes about the environment around it.

In this lecture, we'll cover:

@itemize
@item
@ref{Shipping - Dependencies & Environments,,Dependencies & Environments}
@item
@ref{Shipping - Artifacts & Packaging,,Artifacts & Packaging}
@item
@ref{Shipping - Releases & Versioning,,Releases & Versioning}
@item
@ref{Shipping - Reproducibility,,Reproducibility}
@item
@ref{Shipping - VMs & Containers,,VMs & Containers}
@item
@ref{Shipping - Configuration,,Configuration}
@item
@ref{Shipping - Services & Orchestration,,Services & Orchestration}
@item
@ref{Shipping - Publishing,,Publishing}
@end itemize

We'll explain these concepts through examples from the Python ecosystem, as concrete examples are helpful for understanding. While the tools are different for other programming language ecosystems, the concepts will largely be the same.

