@heading Study guide (added for this edition)

This lecture explains how a coding agent combines a language model, conversation history, and tools. The main skill is to give the agent a clear task and a useful feedback loop. You remain responsible for the result. Read this lecture after the development environment lecture, then compare the different ways an agent can help with the same small change.

@subheading Learning objectives
@itemize @bullet
@item
Explain how a harness sends tool results and conversation history back to a model.
@item
Give an agent a task that it can check with a compiler, linter, type checker, or test.
@item
Choose between project instructions, skills, subagents, and separate worktrees.
@item
Manage context and review the changes before you accept the result.
@end itemize

@subheading Reading notes
@itemize @bullet
@item
The opening Python program is a demonstration. Its regular expression finds a limited Markdown link form; it is not a complete Markdown parser.
@item
Keep tool access, model inference, and the context window separate in your reasoning. A local command-line interface can still send data to a remote model.
@item
Product features and model limits change. Treat the named tools and commands as the lecture's January 2026 examples.
@end itemize

@heading Original lecture notes

Coding agents are conversational AI models with access to tools such as reading/writing files, web search, and invoking shell commands. They live either in the IDE or in standalone command-line or GUI tools. Coding agents are highly autonomous and powerful tools, enabling a wide variety of use cases.

This lecture builds on the AI-powered development material from the @ref{Development,,Development Environment and Tools} lecture. As a quick demo, let's continue with the example from the @ref{Development - AI-powered development,,AI-powered development} section:

@example
from urllib.request import urlopen

def download_contents(url: str) -> str:
    with urlopen(url) as response:
        return response.read().decode('utf-8')

def extract(content: str) -> list[str]:
    import re
    pattern = r'\[.*?\]\((.*?)\)'
    return re.findall(pattern, content)

print(extract(download_contents("https://raw.githubusercontent.com/missing-semester/missing-semester/refs/heads/master/_2026/development-environment.md")))
@end example

We can try prompting a coding agent with the following task:

@example
Turn this into a proper command-line program, with argparse for argument parsing. Add type annotations, and make sure the program passes type checking.
@end example

The agent will read the file to understand it, then make some edits, and finally invoke the type checker to make sure the type annotations are correct. If it makes a mistake such that it fails type checking, it will likely iterate, though this is a simple task so that is unlikely to happen. Because coding agents have access to tools that may be harmful, by default, agent harnesses prompt the user to confirm tool calls.

@quotation
If the coding agent makes a mistake --- for example, if you have the @code{mypy} binary available directly on @code{$PATH} but the agent tries calling @code{python -m mypy} --- you can give it text feedback to help it course correct.
@end quotation
Coding agents support multi-turn interaction, so you can iterate on work over a back-and-forth conversation with the agent. You can even interrupt the agent if it's going down the wrong track. One helpful mental model might be that of a manager of an intern: the intern will do the nitty gritty work, but will require guidance, and will occasionally do the wrong thing and need to be corrected.

@quotation
For a more illustrative demo, try asking the agent as a follow-up to run the resulting script. Observe the outputs, and try asking it to make a change (e.g., ask it to include only absolute URLs).
@end quotation

