@heading Study guide (added for this edition)

This lecture treats technical communication as part of software engineering. Good comments, commit messages, bug reports, and reviews preserve the reasons behind a decision. They also reduce the work that another person must do to understand your contribution. Use the exercises with a project you know, so you can compare the written record with the context that the author probably had.

@subheading Learning objectives
@itemize @bullet
@item
Write comments that record assumptions, constraints, and reasons that the code does not show.
@item
Explain a change through its problem, solution, and consequences.
@item
Prepare a bug report with an environment, expected result, actual result, and reproducible steps.
@item
Give specific review feedback and distinguish required changes from optional suggestions.
@item
Ask focused questions and disclose meaningful AI assistance.
@end itemize

@subheading Reading notes
@itemize @bullet
@item
Keep the reader's task in view. A useful explanation supplies missing context without repeating each operation in the code.
@item
Read the project's contribution instructions before you prepare a change. A small, coherent patch is easier to explain and review.
@item
The exercises ask you to inspect real examples. Record the evidence for your judgment, including what a maintainer would still need to ask.
@end itemize

@heading Original lecture notes

Being a good software engineer isn't just about writing code that works. It's about writing code that others (including future you) can understand, maintain, and build upon. It's about communicating clearly, contributing thoughtfully, and being a good citizen in the ecosystems you participate in---whether open source or proprietary.

