@node Beyond Code - One-way communication, Beyond Code - Collaboration, Beyond Code, Beyond Code
@unnumberedsec One-way communication
@cindex One-way communication

Much of software engineering involves writing for people who lack your current context: teammates who join later, maintainers who inherit your code, or yourself in six months when you've forgotten why you made a particular choice. A key piece of advice for all this kind of writing is that your goal is to capture and convey the @emph{why}, not just the @emph{what}. The what tends to be self-explanatory, while the @emph{why} is hard-earned knowledge that is easily lost to time.

Perhaps the most common form of engineer-to-engineer communication (apart from the code itself) is code comments. I've personally found that a lot of code comments are useless. But they don't have to be! Good comments explain things that the code itself cannot: @emph{why} something is done a particular way, not @emph{how} it works (which is what the code shows). They can save hours of confusion, while bad comments add noise or, worse, mislead.

Types of comments that are nearly always worthwhile:

@itemize
@item
@strong{TODOs}: Mark incomplete or unpolished code, but leave enough context for someone else to understand what's outstanding and why it was deferred. "TODO: optimize" is useless; "TODO: this O(n²) loop is fine for @code{n<100}, but will need indexing if we scale" is actionable.
@item
@strong{References}: Link to external sources when code implements an algorithm from a paper, adapts code from elsewhere, or encodes behaviour specified in documentation. Use permalinks. Note any divergences from the reference.
@item
@strong{Correctness arguments}: Explain @emph{why} non-trivial code produces correct results. The code shows the steps; a comment explains why those steps work.
@item
@strong{Hard-learned lessons}: If you spent 30+ minutes debugging something and the fix is a non-obvious incantation, document it. Your past self didn't realize it was needed; future readers won't either.
@item
@strong{Rationale for constants}: Magic numbers deserve explanation. Why 1492? Why 16 bits? Was it chosen randomly, derived from testing, or required for correctness? Even "chosen arbitrarily" is useful information.
@item
@strong{Load-bearing choices}: If correctness depends on a seemingly-innocent implementation detail (e.g., "must be a BTreeSet because iteration order matters below"), call it out explicitly.
@item
@strong{"Why not"s}: When you deliberately avoid the obvious approach, explain why. Otherwise someone will "fix" it later and break things.
@end itemize

READMEs (you have one, right?) are also a common first touch-point with other developers. A good one answers four questions immediately: What does this do? Why should I care? How do I use it? How do I install it? In that order. Structure it like a funnel: a one-liner and maybe a visual demo at the top so someone can decide in seconds if this solves their problem, then progressively add depth. Show usage before installation --- people want to see what they're getting before committing to setup steps.

Commit messages are another kind of "writing for others" that is often neglected. They are often written as "fixed blah" or "added foo", and while that may be sufficient in some cases, it's easy to forget that they form the historical record of @emph{why} the codebase evolved the way it did. When someone (including you!) runs @code{git blame} trying to understand a confusing change, good commit messages should give them answers.

In general, the body should answer:

@itemize
@item
What problem forced this change?
@item
What alternatives did you consider?
@item
What are the trade-offs or implications?
@item
What might be surprising about this approach?
@end itemize

@quotation
Obviously you should scale detail with complexity. A one-line typo fix needs only a subject. A subtle race condition fix that took hours to debug deserves paragraphs explaining the problem and solution.
@end quotation
For complex changes, it can be useful to follow a Problem → Solution → Implications structure: Start with the forcing function or limitation, then explain what changed and the key design decisions, and then list noteworthy consequences (positive and negative). That last part is particularly important; real engineering involves balancing concerns, and documenting that a trade-off was intentional prevents future developers from thinking you missed the problem.

LLMs @emph{can} be helpful in writing commit messages. However, if you simply point one at your change and ask it to write the commit message for the change, the LLM will only have access to the @emph{what}, not the @emph{why}. And the resulting commit message will thus be mostly descriptive (the opposite of what we want!). If you used an LLM to help you make the change in the first place, asking the LLM to write the commit in that same session can be a much better option since your conversation with the LLM is inherently a rich source of context about the change! Otherwise, or in addition, a useful trick is to specifically tell the LLM you'd like a commit message focused on the "why" (and other nuances from the notes above), and then @emph{tell it to query you for missing context}. Essentially, you're acting like a MCP "tool" for the coding agent that it can use to "read" context.

As your changes get more complex, make sure to also break up commits logically (@code{git add -p} is your friend). Each commit should represent one coherent change that could be understood and reviewed independently. Don't mix refactoring with new features or combine unrelated bug fixes, as this muddies the story for which changes fixed what problem, and will almost certainly slow down the eventual review of your changes. It also gives you superpowers through @code{git bisect}, but that's a story for another time.

@quotation
One note as you start being more diligent about technical writing, and using it more extensively, make sure you respect the reader. It's easy to end up over-explaining once you start, but you have to resist that urge lest the reader read @emph{none} of what you've written. Explain the "why" and trust them to figure out the "how" for their situation.
@end quotation

@node Beyond Code - Collaboration, Beyond Code - Education, Beyond Code - One-way communication, Beyond Code
@unnumberedsec Collaboration
@cindex Collaboration

As engineers, we may spend a large part of our job coding at our own keyboard, but a sizeable chunk of our time is also taken up by communicating with others. That time is usually split into collaboration and education, and the payoff from investing in getting better at both is significant.

@menu
* Beyond Code - Contributing:: Contributing
* Beyond Code - Reviewing:: Reviewing
@end menu

@node Beyond Code - Contributing, Beyond Code - Reviewing, Beyond Code - Collaboration, Beyond Code - Collaboration
@unnumberedsubsec Contributing
@cindex Contributing

Whether you are submitting a bug report, contributing a simple bug fix, or implementing a huge feature, it's worth keeping in mind that there are usually orders of magnitude more users than there are contributors, and an order of magnitude more contributors than there are maintainers. As a result, maintainer time is highly oversubscribed. If you want to increase the likelihood that your contribution goes somewhere productive, you have to ensure that your contributions carry a high signal-to-noise ratio and are worth the maintainers' time.

For example, a good bug report respects the maintainer's time by providing everything needed to understand and reproduce the problem:

@itemize
@item
@strong{Environment}: OS, version numbers, relevant configuration
@item
@strong{What you expected} vs @strong{what actually happened}
@item
@strong{Steps to reproduce}: Be specific. "Click the button" is less useful than "Click the Submit button on the /settings page while logged in as an admin."
@item
@strong{What you've already tried}: This prevents duplicate suggestions and shows you've done some investigation
@end itemize

@quotation
If you find a security vulnerability, don't post it publicly. Contact the maintainers privately first and give them reasonable time to fix it before disclosure. Many projects have a SECURITY.md file or similar for this purpose.
@end quotation
@strong{Make sure you search for existing issues.} Your bug or feature request may already be reported, and it's far better to add information to existing discussions rather than creating duplicates. Not to mention, it reduces noise for the maintainers.

Minimal reproducible examples are gold, if you can come up with one. They save the maintainer a huge amount of time and effort, and reliably reproducing the bug is often the hardest part of fixing it. Not to mention, the effort you put into isolating the problem often helps you understand it better too, and sometimes leads you to find a fix yourself.

If you don't hear back right away, keep in mind that maintainers are often volunteers with limited time. If you're waiting for a reply from them, a polite follow-up after a couple weeks is fine; daily pings are not. Similarly, "me too" comments, or bug reports that are just a copy-paste of some terminal output tend to be a net-negative in terms of getting traction for your issue.

If you're looking to make a code contribution, you'll also want to familiarize yourself with the contribution guidelines. Many projects have a @code{CONTRIBUTING.md} --- follow it. You'll also usually want to start small; a typo fix or documentation improvement is a great first contribution as it helps you learn the project's processes without also having to go through lots of back and forth on the content.

@quotation
Check what license the project uses, as any code you contribute will fall under the same license. In particular, look out for copyleft licenses (like GPL), which requires derivatives to also be open source and may have implications for your employer if you touch it! @uref{https://choosealicense.com/,choosealicense.com} has more useful information.
@end quotation
When you've decided to open a pull request ("PR"), first make sure you isolate the change you actually want to be accepted. If your PR changes lots of other unrelated things at the same time, chances are the reviewer will send it back to you asking you to clean it up. This is similar to how you should break down your git commits into semantically related chunks.

In some cases, if you have many seemingly-disparate changes but they're all needed to enable one feature, it may be okay to open a larger PR that captures all the changes. However, in this case, commit hygiene is particularly important so that maintainers have the option to review the change "commit by commit".

Next, make sure you explain the "why" behind the change well. Don't just describe @emph{what} changed --- explain @emph{why} the change is needed and @emph{why} this is a good way to address the problem. You should also proactively call out parts of the change that warrant special attention in the review, if any. Depending on @code{CONTRIBUTING.md} and the nature of your change, reviewers may also expect to see additional information like trade-offs you made or how to test the change.

@quotation
We recommend contributing back to upstream projects rather than "forking" the project, at least as a first approach. Forking (license permitting) should be reserved for when the contributions you want to make are out of scope for the original project. If you do fork, make sure you acknowledge the original project!
@end quotation
AI makes it incredibly easy to generate plausible-looking code and PRs quickly, but this doesn't excuse you from understanding what you're contributing. Submitting AI-generated code you can't explain burdens maintainers with reviewing and potentially maintaining code that even its author doesn't understand. It's fine to use AI to help you identify issues and produce fixes/features, @strong{so long as you still do the due diligence} to polish it into a worthwhile contribution, rather than passing that work on to the (already-overloaded) maintainers.

Remember that for maintainers, accepting a PR means accepting long-term responsibility. They will be maintaining this code long after the contributor has moved on, and so may decline changes that are well-intentioned but don't fit the project's direction, add complexity they don't want to maintain, or where the need simply isn't sufficiently well-documented. It's on @emph{you} as the contributor to make the case for why accepting the contribution is worth the maintenance burden.

@quotation
When receiving feedback on a PR, remember that your code is not you! Reviewers are trying to make the code better, not criticizing you personally. Ask clarifying questions if you disagree --- you might learn something, or maybe they will.
@end quotation

@node Beyond Code - Reviewing, , Beyond Code - Contributing, Beyond Code - Collaboration
@unnumberedsubsec Reviewing
@cindex Reviewing

You might think code review is something senior developers do, but you'll likely be asked to review code much earlier than you expect, and your perspective is valuable. Fresh eyes catch things that experienced developers overlook, and questions from someone less familiar with the code often reveal assumptions that should be documented or simplified.

Review is also one of the fastest ways to learn. You'll see how others approach problems, pick up patterns and idioms, and develop intuition for what makes code readable. Beyond personal growth, reviews catch bugs before they reach production, spread knowledge across the team, and improve code quality through collaboration. They are not merely bureaucratic overhead.

Good code review is a skill you need to hone over time, but there are some tips that can make them much better much faster:

@itemize
@item
@strong{Review the code, not the person}: "This function is confusing" vs "You wrote confusing code."
@item
@strong{Prefer actionable comments}: "Can you replace these globals with a config dataclass" is an easier comment to address than "Don't use globals here"
@item
@strong{Ask questions rather than making demands}: "What happens if X is null here?" invites discussion better than "Handle the null case."
@item
@strong{Explain the "why"}: "Consider using a constant here" is less useful than "Consider using a constant here so we can easily adjust the timeout based on environment."
@item
@strong{Distinguish blocking issues from suggestions}: Be clear about what must change versus what's a matter of preference.
@item
@strong{Acknowledge what's good}: Pointing out clever solutions or clean implementations is encouraging and helps the author know what to continue doing.
@item
@strong{Know when to stop}: Contributors only have so much time and patience, and it's not always best spent handling all the nits. Focus on the big things, and consider tidying up nits yourself after the fact.
@end itemize

@quotation
AI tools can catch certain issues, but they're not a substitute for human review. They miss context, don't understand product requirements, and can confidently suggest wrong things. They're worth using as a first pass, but not a replacement for thoughtful human review.
@end quotation

@node Beyond Code - Education, Beyond Code - AI etiquette, Beyond Code - Collaboration, Beyond Code
@unnumberedsec Education
@cindex Education

A lot of our non-coding time as engineers is spent either asking or answering questions, possibly a mixture of both; during collaboration, in dialogue with peers, or while trying to learn. Asking good questions is a skill that makes you better at learning from anyone, not just perfect explainers. Julia Evans has some excellent blog posts on "@uref{https://jvns.ca/blog/good-questions/,How to ask good questions}" and "@uref{https://jvns.ca/blog/2021/10/21/how-to-get-useful-answers-to-your-questions/,How to get useful answers to your questions}" that are worth reading.

Some particularly valuable pieces of advice are:

@itemize
@item
@strong{State your understanding first}: Say what you think you know and ask "is that right?" This helps the answerer identify your actual knowledge gaps.
@item
@strong{Ask yes/no questions}: "Is X true?" prevents tangential explanations and usually prompts useful elaboration anyway.
@item
@strong{Be specific}: "How do SQL joins work?" is too vague. "Does a LEFT JOIN include rows where the right table has no match?" is answerable.
@item
@strong{Admit when you don't understand}: Interrupt to ask about unfamiliar terms. This reflects confidence, not weakness. Similarly, if they ask questions of you that you do not know the answer to, it's best to say "I don't know", and possibly follow up with "but I think ..." or even "but I can find out".
@item
@strong{Don't accept incomplete answers}: Keep asking follow-ups until you actually understand.
@item
@strong{Do some research first}: Basic investigation helps you ask more targeted questions (though casual questions among colleagues are fine).
@end itemize

Remember: well-crafted questions benefit entire communities. They surface hidden assumptions that others need to understand too.

@quotation
Note that this advice applies just as much when communicating with LLMs!
@end quotation

@node Beyond Code - AI etiquette, Beyond Code - Exercises, Beyond Code - Education, Beyond Code
@unnumberedsec AI etiquette
@cindex AI etiquette

With the growing use of LLMs and AI across software engineering, the social and professional norms around are still in flux. We already covered many of the tactical considerations in the @ref{Agents,,agentic coding lecture}, but there are also "softer" parts of their use that are worth discussing.

The first of these is that when AI meaningfully contributed to your work, @strong{disclose it}. This isn't about shame --- it's about honesty, setting appropriate expectations, and ensuring the resulting work gets the appropriate level of review. It's also worthwhile to disclose which @emph{parts} you use AI for --- there's a meaningful distinction between "this whole thing is vibecoded" and "I wrote this backup tool and used an LLM to style the web frontend". For example, we've used LLMs to help write some of these lecture notes, including proofreading, brainstorming, and generating first drafts of code snippets and exercises.

You'll also want to follow the norms of the teams and projects you're contributing to here. Some teams have stricter policies around the use of AI than others (e.g., for compliance or data residency reasons), and you don't want to accidentally run afoul of that. Being open about your use helps prevent potentially costly mistakes.

@quotation
If you're aiming to learn as part of the work you're doing, keep in mind that if you have AI do all or most of the work for you can be self-defeating; you're likely to learn more about prompting (and maybe reviewing AI output) than the task itself. Especially when you're learning, the point may be the journey, not the destination, so using AI to "get the solution quickly" is an anti-goal.
@end quotation
A related concern comes up in interviews and other assessment situations. These are often intended to specifically evaluate @emph{your} skills and abilities, not those of an LLM. More companies now allow you to use LLMs and other AI-assisted tooling in interviews as long as you let them observe those interactions as part of the interview (i.e., they are evaluating your skill in making use of those tools too!), but those are still in the minority. If you are unsure about whether AI assistance is in scope for a particular task, ask!

@quotation
It should go without saying that if an assessment situation explicitly calls for no external tools, no LLMs, etc., you should not use them. Trying to do so discretely without getting caught @strong{will} come back to bite you.
@end quotation

@node Beyond Code - Exercises, , Beyond Code - AI etiquette, Beyond Code
@unnumberedsec Exercises
@cindex Exercises

@enumerate
@item
Browse the source code of a well-known project (e.g., @uref{https://github.com/redis/redis,Redis} or @uref{https://github.com/curl/curl,curl}). Find examples of some of the comment types mentioned in the lecture: a useful TODO, a reference to external documentation, a "why not" comment explaining an avoided approach, or a hard-learned lesson. What would be lost if that comment was not there?

@item
Pick an open-source project you're interested in and look at its recent commit history (@code{git log}). Find one commit with a good message that explains @emph{why} the change was made, and one with a weak message that only describes @emph{what} changed. For the weak one, look at the diff (@code{git show <hash>}) and try to write a better commit message following the Problem → Solution → Implications structure. Notice how much work is required to reassemble the necessary context after the fact!

@item
Compare the READMEs of three GitHub projects with 1000+ stars. Are all of them equally useful? Look for things that come across mostly as noise to you as a lesson for future READMEs you write yourself.

@item
Find an open issue on a project you use (check the "good first issue" or "help wanted" labels if they have it). Evaluate the issue against the criteria from the lecture: does it seem like it values the maintainer's time and contains all the information necessary to debug it, or do you expect that the maintainer may need to go multiple rounds of questions with the submitter to get to the root problem?

@item
Think of a bug you've encountered in software you use (or find one in an issue tracker). Practice creating a minimal reproducible example: strip away everything unrelated to the bug until you have the smallest case that still demonstrates the problem. Write up what you removed and why.

@item
Find a merged pull request on a project you're familiar with that has substantive review comments (not just "LGTM"). Read through the review. Were all the comments equally productive? If you were the PR author, how would you find the experience of getting all those comments?

@item
Go to Stack Overflow and find a question in a technology you know that has a highly-voted answer. Then find one that was closed or heavily downvoted. Compare them against the advice from the lecture; was it predictable which question would get better answers?

@end enumerate

@node Quality, Sources and License, Beyond Code, Top
@unnumbered 9. Code Quality
@cindex code formatting
@cindex static analysis
@cindex unit tests
@cindex property-based testing
@cindex capture groups

Lecture date: 2026-01-23.

@uref{https://missing.csail.mit.edu/2026/code-quality/,Original notes}. @uref{https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XBiLUNx84CQ,Lecture video}.

@heading Study guide (added for this edition)

This lecture connects local code checks with repeatable project checks. Formatters standardize syntax, linters detect selected problems, and tests check observed behavior. Hooks and continuous integration run these tools at useful points in development. The final sections introduce regular expressions as a shared tool for search, replacement, and test selection. Work through a small project so you can see both a passing check and an intentional failure.

@subheading Learning objectives
@itemize @bullet
@item
Distinguish formatting, static analysis, tests, and coverage.
@item
Configure checks that contributors can run locally and in continuous integration.
@item
Explain the roles of pre-commit hooks, command runners, and deployment jobs.
@item
Use regex groups and quantifiers, and recognize when a parser is the better tool.
@end itemize

@subheading Reading notes
@itemize @bullet
@item
Coverage shows which code ran. It does not show whether an assertion checked the correct result or whether every useful input was tested.
@item
Regex syntax depends on the engine. The lecture uses Python syntax; grep and an editor can use different rules and replacement references.
@item
In Python, a dot excludes a newline by default. Digit and word classes include Unicode characters unless an appropriate flag changes that behavior.
@item
Read prompt examples and interpreter transcripts as examples; do not include their surrounding prose or prompt markers in a program.
@end itemize

@heading Original lecture notes

There are a variety of tools and techniques that support developers in writing high-quality code. In this lecture, we'll cover:

@itemize
@item
@ref{Quality - Formatting,,Formatting}
@item
@ref{Quality - Linting,,Linting}
@item
@ref{Quality - Testing,,Testing}
@item
@ref{Quality - Pre-commit hooks,,Pre-commit hooks}
@item
@ref{Quality - Continuous integration,,Continuous integration}
@item
@ref{Quality - Command runners,,Command runners}
@end itemize

As a bonus topic, we'll also cover @ref{Quality - Regular expressions,,regular expressions}, a cross-cutting topic that has applications in code quality (e.g., for running a subset of tests that match a pattern) as well as other domains like IDEs (e.g., for search and replace).

Many of these tools will be language-specific (e.g., the @uref{https://docs.astral.sh/ruff/,Ruff} linter/formatter for Python). In some cases, tools will support multiple languages (e.g., the @uref{https://prettier.io/,Prettier} code formatter). The concepts, however, are near universal --- you can find code formatters, linters, testing libraries, and so on for any programming language.

@menu
* Quality - Formatting:: Formatting
* Quality - Linting:: Linting
* Quality - Testing:: Testing
* Quality - Pre-commit hooks:: Pre-commit hooks
* Quality - Continuous integration:: Continuous integration
* Quality - Command runners:: Command runners
* Quality - Regular expressions:: Regular expressions
* Quality - Exercises:: Exercises
@end menu

@node Quality - Formatting, Quality - Linting, Quality, Quality
@unnumberedsec Formatting
@cindex Formatting

Code auto-formatters automatically prettify surface syntax. This way, you can focus on the more deep and challenging problems, while the auto-formatting tool handles mundane details such as consistency of @code{'} versus @code{"} syntax for strings, having spaces surrounding binary operators (@code{x + y} instead of @code{x+y}), having @code{import} statements in sorted order, and avoiding over-length lines. One major benefit of code formatters is that they standardize code style across all developers working on a codebase.

Some tools such as Prettier are @uref{https://prettier.io/docs/configuration,highly configurable}; you should check in the configuration file into @ref{Git,,version control} for your project. Other tools, such as @uref{https://github.com/psf/black,Black} and @uref{https://pkg.go.dev/cmd/gofmt,gofmt} have limited or no configurability, to reduce @uref{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Law_of_triviality,bikeshedding}.

You can set up @ref{Development - Code intelligence and language servers,,IDE integration} with your code formatter, so that your code will be auto-formatted as you type or when you save a file. You can also add an @uref{https://editorconfig.org/,EditorConfig} file to your project, which communicates to your IDE certain project-level settings like indent size for each file type.

@node Quality - Linting, Quality - Testing, Quality - Formatting, Quality
@unnumberedsec Linting
@cindex Linting

Linters run static analysis (analyze your code without running it) to find antipatterns and potential issues in your code. These tools go deeper than autoformatters, looking beyond surface syntax. The depth of analysis varies by tool.

Linters come equipped with lists of @emph{rules}, with presets that can be configured on a project-level basis. Some linter rules produce false positives, so you can disable them on a per-file or per-line basis.

Good linters will have built-in help or documentation that explains each linter rule --- what the rule is looking for, why it's bad, and what's a better alternative for the code pattern. For example, see the documentation for the @uref{https://docs.astral.sh/ruff/rules/collapsible-if/,SIM102} rule in @uref{https://docs.astral.sh/ruff/,Ruff} which catches unnecessarily nested @code{if} statements in Python code.

Some linters can not only flag issues but also automatically fix certain issues for you.

Aside from language-specific linters, another tool that might come in handy is @uref{https://github.com/semgrep/semgrep,semgrep}, a "semantic grep" tool that works at the AST level (rather than character level, like grep) and supports many languages. You can use semgrep to easily write custom linter rules for your projects. For example, if you wanted to prevent the dangerous @code{subprocess.Popen(..., shell=True)} in Python, you could find that code pattern with:

@example
semgrep -l python -e "subprocess.Popen(..., shell=True, ...)"
@end example

@node Quality - Testing, Quality - Pre-commit hooks, Quality - Linting, Quality
@unnumberedsec Testing
@cindex Testing

Software testing is a standard technique to increase your confidence in the correctness of your code. You write code, and then you write code that exercises the code you wrote and raises an error if the code doesn't work as expected.

You can write tests for chunks of code at different levels of granularity: @emph{unit tests} for individual functions, @emph{integration tests} for interaction between modules or services, and @emph{functional tests} for end-to-end scenarios. You can do @emph{test-driven development}, where you write tests before you write any implementation code. When you find bugs in your code, you can write @emph{regression tests}, so you'll catch if the functionality ever breaks in the future. You can write @emph{property-based tests}, pioneered in @uref{https://hackage.haskell.org/package/QuickCheck,QuickCheck} in Haskell, and implemented in many libraries, like @uref{https://hypothesis.readthedocs.io/,Hypothesis} for Python. Which approach to testing is right depends on your project; likely, you will adopt some combination.

If your program has external dependencies like a database or web API, it may be helpful to @emph{mock} those dependencies in your tests, rather than have your code interact with third-party dependencies at test time.

@menu
* Quality - Code coverage:: Code coverage
@end menu

@node Quality - Code coverage, , Quality - Testing, Quality - Testing
@unnumberedsubsec Code coverage
@cindex Code coverage

Code coverage is a metric by which you can measure how good your tests are. Code coverage looks at which lines of your code are executed when your tests are run, so you can ensure you are covering all code paths. Code coverage tools can show you line-by-line coverage to guide you in writing tests. Services such as @uref{https://app.codecov.io,Codecov} provide web interfaces for tracking and viewing code coverage over the history of a project.

Like any metric, code coverage is not perfect; don't over-index on coverage, focus on writing high-quality tests.

@node Quality - Pre-commit hooks, Quality - Continuous integration, Quality - Testing, Quality
@unnumberedsec Pre-commit hooks
@cindex Pre-commit hooks

Git pre-commit @uref{https://git-scm.com/book/en/v2/Customizing-Git-Git-Hooks,hooks}, made easier by the @uref{https://pre-commit.com/,pre-commit} framework, automatically run user-specified code prior to every Git commit. Projects commonly use pre-commit hooks to run formatters and linters, and sometimes tests, automatically before every commit, to ensure that committed code matches the project code style and is free of certain issues.

@node Quality - Continuous integration, Quality - Command runners, Quality - Pre-commit hooks, Quality
@unnumberedsec Continuous integration
@cindex Continuous integration

Continuous integration (CI) services like @uref{https://github.com/features/actions,GitHub Actions} can run scripts for you every time you push code (or on every pull request, or on a schedule). Developers commonly use CI services to run code quality tools including formatters, linters, and tests. For compiled languages, you can ensure code compiles; for statically typed languages, you can make sure it type checks. Running CI every push of new commits can catch errors introduced into the main version of the code; running on pull requests can catch issues with contributor submissions; running on a schedule can catch issues with external dependencies (e.g., a developer accidentally releases a breaking change as @ref{Shipping - Releases & Versioning,,semver-compatible}).

Because CI scripts run separately from developer machines, you can easily run long-running jobs there. This can be leveraged, for example, to run a @emph{matrix} of tests across different operating systems and programming language versions to ensure that the software works properly across all of them.

Generally, the script running in CI will not directly make changes to your code: it will run tools in "check-only" mode rather than "fix" mode, so for example, the auto-formatter will raise an error when the code is not compliant with the format.

Repositories often include @uref{https://docs.github.com/en/actions/how-tos/monitor-workflows/add-a-status-badge,status badges} in their README, showing CI status and other information such as code coverage. For example, below is Missing Semester's current build status.

@uref{https://github.com/missing-semester/missing-semester/actions/workflows/build.yml,Build Status} @uref{https://github.com/missing-semester/missing-semester/actions/workflows/links.yml,Links Status}

@quotation
Our @uref{https://github.com/missing-semester/missing-semester/blob/master/.github/workflows/links.yml,links checker}, which uses the @uref{https://github.com/anishathalye/proof-html,proof-html} GitHub Action is often failing, usually due to issues with third-party websites. Still, it has helped us catch and fix many broken links (sometimes due to typos, most of the time due to websites moving around content without adding redirects or websites disappearing).
@end quotation
A good way to learn the particulars of CI services, formatters, linters, and testing libraries is by example. Find high-quality open-source projects on GitHub---the more similar to your project in programming language, domain, size and scope, and so on, the better---and study their @code{pyproject.toml}, @code{.github/workflows/}, @code{DEVELOPMENT.md}, and other relevant files.

@menu
* Quality - Continuous deployment:: Continuous deployment
@end menu

@node Quality - Continuous deployment, , Quality - Continuous integration, Quality - Continuous integration
@unnumberedsubsec Continuous deployment
@cindex Continuous deployment

Continuous deployment makes use of CI infrastructure to actually @emph{deploy} changes. For example, the Missing Semester repository uses continuous deployment to GitHub pages so that whenever we @code{git push} updated lecture notes, the site is automatically built and deployed. You can build other types of @ref{Shipping,,artifacts} in CI, such as binaries for applications or Docker images for services.

@node Quality - Command runners, Quality - Regular expressions, Quality - Continuous integration, Quality
@unnumberedsec Command runners
@cindex Command runners

Command runners like @uref{https://github.com/casey/just,just} simplify the task of running commands in the context of a project. As you build up code quality infrastructure for your project, you don't want to make your developers memorize commands like @code{uv run ruff check --fix}. With a command runner, this can turn into @code{just lint}, and you can have analogous invocations like @code{just format}, @code{just typecheck}, etc., for all the different tools a developer might want to run for your project.

Some language-specific project or package managers have built-in support for such functionality, which means you don't need to use a language-agnostic tool like @code{just}. For example, the @code{scripts} section of a @code{package.json} for @uref{https://nodejs.org/en/learn/getting-started/an-introduction-to-the-npm-package-manager,npm} (Node.js) and the @code{tool.hatch.envs.*.scripts} sections of a @code{pyproject.toml} for @uref{https://hatch.pypa.io/,Hatch} (Python) support this functionality.

@node Quality - Regular expressions, Quality - Exercises, Quality - Command runners, Quality
@unnumberedsec Regular expressions
@cindex Regular expressions

@emph{Regular expressions}, commonly abbreviated as "regex", is a language used to represent sets of strings. Regex patterns are commonly used for pattern matching in various contexts such as command-line tools and IDEs. For example, @uref{https://github.com/ggreer/the_silver_searcher,ag} supports regex patterns for codebase-wide search (e.g., @code{ag "import .* as .*"} will find all renamed imports in Python), and @uref{https://pkg.go.dev/cmd/go#hdr-Test_packages,go test} supports a @code{-run [regexp]} option for selecting a subset of tests. Furthermore, programming languages have built-in support or third-party libraries for regular expression matching, so you can use regexes for functionality such as pattern matching, validation, and parsing.

To help build intuition, below are some examples of regex patterns. In this lecture, we use @uref{https://docs.python.org/3/library/re.html,Python regex syntax}. There are many flavors of regex, with slight variation between them, especially in the more sophisticated functionality. You can use an online regex tester like @uref{https://regex101.com/,regex101} to develop and debug regular expressions.

@itemize
@item
@code{abc} --- matches the literal "abc".
@item
@code{missing|semester} --- matches the string "missing" or the string "semester".
@item
@code{\d@{4@}-\d@{2@}-\d@{2@}} --- matches dates in YYYY-MM-DD format, such as "2026-01-14". Beyond ensuring that the string consists of four digits, a dash, two digits, a dash, and two digits, this does not validate the date, so "2026-01-99" matches this regex pattern too.
@item
@code{.+@@.+} --- matches email addresses, strings that contain some text, then an "@@", and then some more text. This does only the most basic validation and matches strings like "nonsense@@@@@@email". A regex that matches email addresses with no false positives or negatives @uref{https://pdw.ex-parrot.com/Mail-RFC822-Address.html,exists} but is impractical.
@end itemize

@menu
* Quality - Regex syntax:: Regex syntax
* Quality - Capture groups and references:: Capture groups and references
* Quality - Limitations:: Limitations
* Quality - Learning regex:: Learning regex
@end menu

@node Quality - Regex syntax, Quality - Capture groups and references, Quality - Regular expressions, Quality - Regular expressions
@unnumberedsubsec Regex syntax
@cindex Regex syntax

You can find a comprehensive guide to regex syntax in @uref{https://docs.python.org/3/library/re.html#regular-expression-syntax,this documentation} (or one of many other resources available online). Here are some of the basic building blocks:

@itemize
@item
@code{abc} matches the literal string, when the characters have no special meaning (in this example, "abc")
@item
@code{.} matches any single character
@item
@code{[abc]} matches a single character contained in the brackets (in this example, "a", "b", or "c")
@item
@code{[^abc]} matches a single character except those contained in the brackets (e.g., "d")
@item
@code{[a-f]} matches a single character contained in the range indicated in the brackets (e.g., "c", but not "q")
@item
@code{a|b} matches either pattern (e.g., "a" or "b")
@item
@code{\d} matches any digit character (e.g., "3")
@item
@code{\w} matches any word character (e.g., "x")
@item
@code{\b} matches any word @emph{boundary} (e.g., in the string "missing semester", matches just before the "m", just after the "g", just before the "s", and just after the "r")
@item
@code{(...)} matches the group of a pattern
@item
@code{...?} matches zero or one of a pattern, such as @code{words?} to match "word" or "words"
@item
@code{...*} matches any number of a pattern, such as @code{.*} to match any number of any character
@item
@code{...+} matches one or more of a pattern, such as @code{\d+} to match any non-zero number of digits
@item
@code{...@{N@}} matches exactly N of a pattern, such as @code{\d@{4@}} for 4 digits
@item
@code{\.} matches a literal "."
@item
@code{\\} matches a literal "\"
@item
@code{^} matches the start of the line
@item
@code{$} matches the end of the line
@end itemize

@node Quality - Capture groups and references, Quality - Limitations, Quality - Regex syntax, Quality - Regular expressions
@unnumberedsubsec Capture groups and references
@cindex Capture groups and references

If you use regex groups @code{(...)}, you can refer to sub-parts of the match for extraction or search-and-replace purposes. For example, to extract just the month from a YYYY-MM-DD style date, you can use the following Python code:

@example
>>> import re
>>> re.match(r"\d@{4@}-(\d@{2@})-\d@{2@}", "2026-01-14").group(1)
'01'
@end example

In your text editor, you can use reference capture groups in replace patterns. The syntax might vary between IDEs. For example, in VS Code, you can use variables like @code{$1}, @code{$2}, etc., and in Vim, you can use @code{\1}, @code{\2}, etc., to reference groups.

@node Quality - Limitations, Quality - Learning regex, Quality - Capture groups and references, Quality - Regular expressions
@unnumberedsubsec Limitations
@cindex Limitations

@uref{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Regular_language,Regular languages} are powerful but limited; there are classes of strings that cannot be expressed as a standard regex (e.g., it is @uref{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pumping_lemma_for_regular_languages,not possible} to write a regular expression that matches the set of strings @{a^n b^n | n ≥ 0@}, the set of strings of a number of "a"s followed by the same number of "b"s; more practically, languages like HTML are not regular languages). In practice, modern regex engines support features like lookahead and backreferences that extend support beyond regular languages, and they are practically extremely useful, but it is important to know that they are still limited in their expressive power. For more sophisticated languages, you might need to reach for a more capable type of parser (for one example, see @uref{https://github.com/pyparsing/pyparsing,pyparsing}, a @uref{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Parsing_expression_grammar,PEG} parser).

@node Quality - Learning regex, , Quality - Limitations, Quality - Regular expressions
@unnumberedsubsec Learning regex
@cindex Learning regex

We recommend learning the fundamentals (what we have covered in this lecture), and then looking at regex references as you need them, rather than memorizing the entirety of the language.

Conversational AI tools can be effective at helping you generating regex patterns. For example, try prompting your favorite LLM with the following query:

@example
Write a Python-style regex pattern that matches the requested path from log lines from Nginx. Here is an example log line:

169.254.1.1 - - [09/Jan/2026:21:28:51 +0000] "GET /feed.xml HTTP/2.0" 200 2995 "-" "python-requests/2.32.3"
@end example

@node Quality - Exercises, , Quality - Regular expressions, Quality
@unnumberedsec Exercises
@cindex Exercises

@enumerate
@item
Configure a formatter, linter, and pre-commit hooks for a project you're working on. If you have lots of errors: autoformatting should take care of the format errors. For the linter errors, try using an @ref{Agents,,AI agent} to fix all the linter errors. Make sure the AI agent can run the linter and observe the results, so that it can run in an iterative loop to fix all the issues. Check the results carefully to ensure the AI doesn't break your code!
@item
Learn a testing library for a language you know and write a unit test for a project you're working on. Run a code coverage tool, generate an HTML-formatted coverage report, and observe the results. Can you find the lines that are covered? Your code coverage will likely be very low. Try manually writing some tests to improve it. Try using an @ref{Agents,,AI agent} to improve coverage; make sure the coding agent can run tests with coverage and produce a line-by-line coverage report, so it knows where to focus. Are the AI-generated tests actually good?
@item
Set up continuous integration to run on every push for a project you're working on. Have CI run formatting, linting, and tests. Break your code on purpose (e.g., introduce a linter violation), and ensure that CI catches it.
@item
Try writing a @ref{Quality - Regular expressions,,regex pattern} and use the @code{grep} @ref{Shell,,command-line tool} to find occurrences of @code{subprocess.Popen(..., shell=True)} in your code. Now, try to "break" the regex pattern. Does @ref{Quality - Linting,,semgrep} still successfully match the dangerous code that trips up your grep invocation?
@item
Practice regex search-and-replace in your IDE or text editor by replacing the @code{-} @uref{https://spec.commonmark.org/0.31.2/#bullet-list-marker,Markdown bullet markers} with @code{*} bullet markers in @uref{https://raw.githubusercontent.com/missing-semester/missing-semester/refs/heads/master/_2026/code-quality.md,these lecture notes}. Note that just replacing all the "-" characters in the file would be incorrect, as there are many uses of that character that are not bullet markers.
@item
Write a regex to capture from JSON structures of the form @code{@{"name": "Alyssa P. Hacker", "college": "MIT"@}} the name (e.g., @code{Alyssa P. Hacker}, in this example). Hint: in your first attempt, you might end up writing a regex that extracts @code{Alyssa P. Hacker", "college": "MIT}; read about greedy quantifiers in the @uref{https://docs.python.org/3/library/re.html,Python regex docs} to figure out how to fix it.
@enumerate
@item
Make the regex pattern work even in situations where the name has a @code{"} character in it (double quotes can be escaped in JSON with @code{\"}).
@item
We do @strong{not} recommend using regular expressions for sophisticated parsing problems in practice. Figure out how to use your programming language's JSON parser for this task. Write a command-line program that takes as input, on stdin, a JSON structure of the form described above, and output, on stdout, the name. You should only need a couple lines of code to do this. In Python, you can do it easily in one line of code beyond @code{import json}.
@end enumerate

@end enumerate

